# 4 — B, kinematics.  A ratio that is independent of the launch speed, so the
#     only thing left is the angle.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 4, "id": "S07-04", "module": "B", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A projectile: the ratio of its maximum height to its horizontal range, which depends on the launch angle alone",
    "rel": [("B", "The maximum height of a projectile is u² sin²θ / 2g"),
            ("B", "The horizontal range over level ground is u² sin 2θ / g, which is 2u² sinθ cosθ / g"),
            ("A", "Dividing one expression by the other, so that u and g cancel and only the angle is left")],
    "key": ["projectile", "range", "height", "ratio"],
    "stem": '<p>A ball is projected from level ground with speed <code>u</code> at an angle <code>&#952;</code> above the horizontal, and lands at the same height, as the diagram shows.</p>{{FIG:s07-04}}<p>Which expression gives the ratio of its maximum height to its horizontal range?</p>',
    "opts": ['tan&#952; / 2',
             'tan&#952; / 4',
             'tan&#952;',
             '2 tan&#952;',
             '4 tan&#952;'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['loses a factor of two by using the range as u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / g rather than twice that, which is the range for the half-journey',
                    'correct',
                    'divides the height by the horizontal distance travelled <i>at the top</i> rather than by the full range, which is the same as omitting the factor of two twice',
                    'inverts the ratio of the two coefficients, giving twice the tangent instead of half of it',
                    'multiplies by four, which comes from treating the range as u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / 2g'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "write the maximum height from the vertical motion alone, using v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as"),
            ("relate", "write the range from the time of flight and the horizontal velocity"),
            ("solve", "expand sin 2&#952; as 2 sin&#952; cos&#952; so that the two expressions share a factor"),
            ("eliminate", "divide, so that u<sup>2</sup> and g cancel and the ratio is a function of the angle only"),
            ("check", "put in 45 degrees and confirm the height is a quarter of the range, which is the familiar case"),
        ],
        "relations": ["H = u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>&#952; / (2g)",
                      "R = u<sup>2</sup> sin 2&#952; / g = 2u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / g",
                      "H/R = sin<sup>2</sup>&#952; / (4 sin&#952; cos&#952;)",
                      "H/R = tan&#952; / 4"],
        "insight": "The speed cancels, and that is the reason the question can be asked at all: whatever the ball is thrown at, the shape of its path is fixed by the angle alone. At 45 degrees the height is a quarter of the range, which is worth carrying in your head as a check on any expression of this kind.",
        "shape": "ratio-cancellation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
        "figure_support": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym",
              "got": "(u**2*sin(t)**2/(2*g)) / (2*u**2*sin(t)*cos(t)/g)",
              "want": "tan(t)/4"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can get two standard expressions into a form where they cancel. Neither formula is hard on its own; the question is whether you notice that the <code>sin 2&#952;</code> in the range has to be expanded before the two will divide.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the maximum height.</b> At the top the vertical velocity is zero, so <code>v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as</code> with <code>v = 0</code> and <code>a = -g</code> gives</p>
<div class="formula">H = u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>&#952; / (2g)</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the range.</b> The time of flight is twice the time to the top, <code>2u sin&#952;/g</code>, and the horizontal velocity is <code>u cos&#952;</code> throughout:</p>
<div class="formula">R = u cos&#952; &#215; 2u sin&#952; / g = 2u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / g</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — divide.</b> The <code>u<sup>2</sup></code> and the <code>g</code> are in both expressions, so they cancel; one power of <code>sin&#952;</code> cancels too:</p>
<div class="formula">H/R = [u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>&#952; / (2g)] &#247; [2u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / g]
    = sin<sup>2</sup>&#952; / (4 sin&#952; cos&#952;)
    = sin&#952; / (4 cos&#952;)
    = tan&#952; / 4</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Put <code>&#952; = 45&#176;</code>. Then <code>tan&#952; = 1</code> and the ratio is 0.25, so the ball rises a quarter as high as it travels. That is the case most people can picture, and it agrees. Now let the angle approach 90 degrees: the ball goes almost straight up, <code>tan&#952;</code> grows without limit, and the range falls to nothing, so the ratio does grow -- which it does. The expression behaves correctly at both ends.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>tan&#952; / 2</b> uses <code>u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / g</code> for the range. That is half the range, the distance covered in the time to the top, and it is the most common slip here.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>tan&#952;</b> divides the height by the horizontal distance at the top of the flight, which drops the factor of two twice.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2 tan&#952;</b> inverts the ratio of the two coefficients, so the division goes the wrong way round.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>4 tan&#952;</b> comes from using <code>u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952; / 2g</code> for the range, which is the half-range with an extra factor of two as well.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the projectile equations; expanding <code>sin 2&#952;</code>; ratios that are independent of the launch speed.</p>''',
    "trap": "Dividing by the half-range. The horizontal distance covered by the time the ball reaches the top is <code>u<sup>2</sup> sin&#952; cos&#952;/g</code>, which is half the range, and using it gives <code>tan&#952;/2</code> -- an answer that looks tidier than the correct one.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 5 — B, kinematics.  A projectile launched horizontally: the angle at impact
#     needs the two velocity components, and one of them comes from a fall.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 5, "id": "S07-05", "module": "B", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A projectile launched horizontally from a height: the tangent of the angle at which it lands",
    "rel": [("B", "The horizontal velocity is unchanged throughout, because gravity has no horizontal component"),
            ("B", "The vertical velocity at the ground comes from the fall through h, not from the launch speed"),
            ("A", "The tangent of the angle below the horizontal is the ratio of the vertical to the horizontal component")],
    "key": ["projectile", "components", "angle", "fall"],
    "stem": '<p>A ball is thrown horizontally with speed <code>u</code> from the top of a cliff of height <code>h</code>, and strikes the ground below. The acceleration due to gravity is <code>g</code>.</p>{{FIG:s07-05}}<p>Which expression gives the tangent of the angle between the ball&#8217;s velocity and the horizontal at the moment it lands?</p>',
    "opts": ['<code>u</code> / &#8730;(2gh)',
             '<code>2gh</code> / <code>u</code>',
             '&#8730;(<code>gh</code>) / <code>u</code>',
             '<code>2</code>&#8730;(<code>gh</code>) / <code>u</code>',
             '&#8730;(<code>2gh</code>) / <code>u</code>'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['inverts the ratio, putting the horizontal component on top, which gives the tangent of the angle above the horizontal instead',
                    'uses the square of the vertical speed, forgetting that the vertical component at impact is a speed and not a speed squared',
                    'drops the factor of two, which is the same as using v<sup>2</sup> = gh instead of v<sup>2</sup> = 2gh for the fall',
                    'keeps a factor of two that belongs inside the square root, so the vertical component is too large by a factor of &#8730;2',
                    'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "note that the horizontal component is unchanged, so it is u at the ground as well"),
            ("relate", "find the vertical component from the fall through h, using v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as with the vertical initial speed zero"),
            ("solve", "form the tangent as vertical over horizontal"),
            ("check", "confirm the angle grows with the height and falls with the launch speed, which is what the expression says"),
        ],
        "relations": ["horizontal component = u",
                      "v_vertical<sup>2</sup> = 0 + 2gh",
                      "tan&#952; = v_vertical / v_horizontal",
                      "tan&#952; = &#8730;(2gh) / u"],
        "insight": "The two components come from different places and that is the point of the question. The horizontal one is handed to you by the launch; the vertical one is earned by the fall, and its size depends on h and not at all on u. So a faster throw gives a shallower landing angle, and the expression says so: u is in the denominator.",
        "shape": "algebraic-elimination",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
        "figure_support": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "sqrt(2*g*h)/u", "want": "sqrt(2*g*h)/u"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can keep the two components of a projectile's velocity separate. The horizontal one never changes; the vertical one is built up by the fall. Students who try to combine them into a single speed lose the angle entirely.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the horizontal component.</b> Gravity acts vertically, so nothing changes the horizontal velocity. At the moment of landing it is still</p>
<div class="formula">v_horizontal = u</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the vertical component.</b> The ball is released with no vertical velocity, so it is a simple fall through a height <code>h</code>. Using <code>v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as</code> with the initial vertical speed zero and <code>a = g</code>:</p>
<div class="formula">v_vertical<sup>2</sup> = 2gh,  so  v_vertical = &#8730;(2gh)</div>
<p>Notice that <code>u</code> does not appear. However hard the ball is thrown, it takes the same time to fall and arrives with the same vertical speed.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the tangent of the angle.</b> The velocity vector points along the sum of the two components, and the tangent of the angle below the horizontal is the vertical component over the horizontal one:</p>
<div class="formula">tan&#952; = v_vertical / v_horizontal = &#8730;(2gh) / u</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Throw it harder and the ball lands shallower: <code>u</code> in the denominator, so a bigger <code>u</code> gives a smaller tangent, which is a smaller angle. Drop it from higher and it lands steeper: <code>h</code> in the numerator. Both are what you would see. And the expression is a pure number, since a speed divided by a speed has no units -- as a tangent must.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>u / &#8730;(2gh)</b> is the reciprocal. It is the tangent of the angle <i>above</i> the horizontal, which is a real angle in this problem, just not the one asked for.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2gh / u</b> uses <code>v<sup>2</sup></code> where <code>v</code> is wanted. The dimensions give it away: <code>2gh</code> has units of m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>, so this is not a pure number.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#8730;(gh) / u</b> uses <code>v<sup>2</sup> = gh</code>, which is the formula for a fall of <code>h/2</code>.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2&#8730;(gh) / u</b> leaves a factor of two inside the root that belongs outside it, so the vertical component is too big by a factor of &#8730;2.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> independence of the horizontal and vertical motions; <code>v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as</code>; resolving a velocity into components.</p>''',
    "trap": "Trying to find the landing <i>speed</i> and then the angle from it, which needs Pythagoras and a square root that never cancels. The tangent is a ratio of the two components, and taking the ratio directly keeps every square root that is needed and none that is not.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 6 — C, momentum.  A perfectly inelastic collision: momentum is conserved and
#     energy is not, and the question asks for the energy.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 6, "id": "S07-06", "module": "C", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A perfectly inelastic collision with a stationary target three times as heavy: the fraction of the kinetic energy lost",
    "rel": [("C", "Momentum is conserved in a collision, because the only forces are internal and act for the same short time"),
            ("C", "Kinetic energy is not conserved when the bodies stick, and that is what 'inelastic' means"),
            ("A", "Comparing the kinetic energy before and after as a ratio, so that the common speed cancels")],
    "key": ["momentum", "inelastic", "energy", "fraction"],
    "stem": '<p>A particle of mass <code>m</code> travelling at speed <code>u</code> strikes a stationary particle of mass <code>3m</code> and the two stick together.</p><p>What fraction of the original kinetic energy is lost in the collision?</p>',
    "opts": ['3/4', '1/4', '1/2', '2/3', '3/8'],
    "ans": 0,
    "distractors": ['correct',
                    'reports the fraction of the kinetic energy that <i>survives</i>, which is the same working read the wrong way round',
                    'assumes half the energy is lost, which is true only when the two masses are equal',
                    'inverts the mass ratio in the energy calculation, giving three parts lost out of two',
                    'takes the lost energy to be the kinetic energy of the heavier particle alone after the collision, which is 1/8 of the original, and then mishandles the remainder'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "use conservation of momentum to find the common speed after the collision"),
            ("solve", "work out the kinetic energy after the collision from that common speed and the total mass"),
            ("solve", "compare it with the kinetic energy before, as a ratio, so that u cancels"),
            ("solve", "subtract the surviving fraction from one to get the fraction lost"),
            ("check", "confirm that the energy lost is positive and smaller than the whole, as it must be"),
        ],
        "relations": ["m u = (m + 3m) v",
                      "v = u/4",
                      "KE_after = &#189; (4m) (u/4)<sup>2</sup> = m u<sup>2</sup>/8",
                      "KE_before = &#189; m u<sup>2</sup>"],
        "insight": "Momentum conservation hands you a speed of one quarter, and then the energy falls as the square of that speed while the mass rises by four. The two effects nearly cancel: the energy that survives is one quarter of one quarter times four, which is one quarter. So three quarters is lost, and the whole thing turns on not confusing the two conservation laws.",
        "shape": "conservation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym",
              "got": "1 - (0.5*(4*m)*(u/4)**2)/(0.5*m*u**2)",
              "want": "3/4"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you keep momentum and energy apart. Momentum is always conserved in a collision; kinetic energy is conserved only when the collision is elastic, and the word "stick" rules that out.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — momentum, before and after.</b> Before, only the moving particle has momentum. After, the two move together with a common speed <code>v</code> and a combined mass <code>4m</code>:</p>
<div class="formula">m u = (m + 3m) v
v = u/4</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the kinetic energy before.</b></p>
<div class="formula">KE_before = &#189; m u<sup>2</sup></div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the kinetic energy after.</b> The mass is four times as big and the speed is four times smaller, so the energy changes by a factor of four from the mass and a factor of sixteen from the speed:</p>
<div class="formula">KE_after = &#189; (4m) (u/4)<sup>2</sup> = &#189; (4m) (u<sup>2</sup>/16) = m u<sup>2</sup>/8</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the fraction lost.</b> Compare with the energy before, which is <code>m u<sup>2</sup>/2</code>:</p>
<div class="formula">KE_after / KE_before = (m u<sup>2</sup>/8) &#247; (m u<sup>2</sup>/2) = 1/4</div>
<p>A quarter survives, so three quarters is lost:</p>
<div class="formula">fraction lost = 1 - 1/4 = 3/4</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: A.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer must be a fraction between zero and one, and 3/4 is. It must also be larger than a half, because the target is three times as heavy as the projectile and a heavy target destroys a lot of the motion. In the limiting case where the target is infinitely heavy the projectile simply stops and everything is lost; as the target gets lighter and lighter, less is lost. So the answer must lie between the equal-mass case and one, and 3/4 does.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>1/4</b> is the fraction that survives. It comes from the same working, stopped one step early -- and the question asks for what is lost, not what is left.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>1/2</b> is the answer when the two masses are equal. It is the case everyone remembers, and it is the wrong case here.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2/3</b> uses the mass ratio the wrong way up in the energy, so that three parts come out lost for every two.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>3/8</b> comes from taking the lost energy to be the heavier particle's share of the final kinetic energy, which is 1/8 of the original, and then mishandling the subtraction.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> conservation of momentum in a perfectly inelastic collision; why kinetic energy is not conserved; the fraction of energy lost as a function of the mass ratio.</p>''',
    "trap": "Answering with the fraction that <i>survives</i>. The working is identical up to the last line, and 1/4 is sitting there as an option.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 7 — C, centre of mass.  A hole is a negative mass, and the only thing that
#     matters is that its moment about the centre is what moves the centre.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 7, "id": "S07-07", "module": "C", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A uniform disc with an off-centre circular hole: where its centre of mass sits, treated as a negative mass",
    "rel": [("C", "The centre of mass of a composite body is the mass-weighted mean of the positions of its parts"),
            ("C", "A hole can be treated as a piece of negative mass, which is what makes the bookkeeping short"),
            ("A", "Working in areas rather than masses, since a uniform sheet has the same density everywhere and it cancels")],
    "key": ["centre of mass", "negative mass", "lamina", "moment"],
    "stem": '<p>A uniform circular disc of radius <code>2a</code> has a circular hole of radius <code>a</code> cut out of it. The centre of the hole is a distance <code>a</code> from the centre of the disc, as the diagram shows.</p>{{FIG:s07-07}}<p>Where is the centre of mass of the remaining shape?</p>',
    "opts": ['A distance <code>a</code>/2 from the centre, on the same side as the hole',
             'A distance <code>a</code>/4 from the centre, on the side away from the hole',
             'A distance <code>a</code>/3 from the centre, on the side away from the hole',
             'A distance <code>a</code>/3 from the centre, on the same side as the hole',
             'A distance <code>2a</code>/3 from the centre, on the side away from the hole'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['puts the centre of mass on the side the material was removed from, which would be right only if the hole had been added rather than cut out',
                    'divides the moment by the area of the whole disc instead of by the area that is left, which is the most common bookkeeping error',
                    'correct',
                    'gets the size right and the direction wrong, so it is the mirror image of the right answer',
                    'treats the hole as having the mass of a disc of radius 2a, which trebles the moment without changing the area'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "treat the shape as a whole disc of radius 2a plus a hole of negative mass of radius a"),
            ("solve", "work out the two areas, since the density is the same everywhere and cancels out of the sum"),
            ("solve", "take moments about the centre of the disc: the whole disc contributes nothing and the hole contributes its negative area times its offset"),
            ("solve", "divide that moment by the area that remains to get the position of the centre of mass"),
            ("check", "confirm the answer is a small distance in the direction the mass was taken from, and that it lies inside the remaining material"),
        ],
        "relations": ["area of the whole disc = &#960;(2a)<sup>2</sup> = 4&#960;a<sup>2</sup>",
                      "area of the hole = &#960;a<sup>2</sup>, so the remaining area is 3&#960;a<sup>2</sup>",
                      "moments about the centre: 4&#960;a<sup>2</sup>(0) - &#960;a<sup>2</sup>(a) = 3&#960;a<sup>2</sup> x",
                      "x = -a/3"],
        "insight": "A hole is a negative mass, and once you see that, the problem is one line of bookkeeping. The whole disc would balance at its centre; removing material from one side is exactly the same as adding material on the other. The only trap is the denominator -- the mass that is left is three quarters of what you started with, not all of it.",
        "shape": "symmetry",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "(4*0 - 1*1)/(4-1)", "want": "-0.3333333333333333"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know that a hole can be carried through the arithmetic as a piece of negative mass. It is the standard trick for composite bodies and it turns a two-dimensional integral into one line.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — replace the hole by a negative mass.</b> A uniform disc with a hole is the same as a complete disc of the full size <i>plus</i> a disc of negative mass where the hole is. The density is the same everywhere, so it cancels and the masses can be replaced by areas.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the areas.</b></p>
<div class="formula">whole disc:  &#960;(2a)<sup>2</sup> = 4&#960;a<sup>2</sup>
hole:        -&#960;a<sup>2</sup>
remaining:   4&#960;a<sup>2</sup> - &#960;a<sup>2</sup> = 3&#960;a<sup>2</sup></div>
<p><b>Step 3 — take moments about the centre of the disc.</b> Measure <code>x</code> positive towards the hole. The whole disc is centred on the origin, so its contribution is zero. The hole is a distance <code>a</code> along the positive direction, so its contribution is its (negative) area times that distance:</p>
<div class="formula">4&#960;a<sup>2</sup> &#215; 0 + (-&#960;a<sup>2</sup>) &#215; a = 3&#960;a<sup>2</sup> &#215; x</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — divide by the area that is left.</b></p>
<div class="formula">-&#960;a<sup>3</sup> = 3&#960;a<sup>2</sup> x
x = -a/3</div>
<p>The minus sign means the centre of mass has moved the other way, <b>away from the hole</b>, by a distance <code>a</code>/3.</p>
<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The remaining material is not symmetric, and the side with less material must be the side the centre of mass moves away from -- which it does. The distance is <code>a</code>/3, which is smaller than <code>a</code>, so the centre of mass is still inside the hole-free part of the disc. And the answer is a length built from the two lengths in the question and nothing else, as it must be.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>a/2, on the same side as the hole</b> has the direction wrong, which would be right only if material had been <i>added</i> there.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>a/4</b> divides the moment by the area of the whole disc, <code>4&#960;a<sup>2</sup></code>, instead of by what remains. It is the same working with the wrong denominator, and it gives a smaller shift than the truth.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>a/3, on the same side as the hole</b> has the size right and the sign wrong: it is the mirror image of the correct answer.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2a/3</b> treats the hole as though its area were four times bigger than it is, trebling the moment without changing the remaining area.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the centre of mass of a composite body; negative mass; moments about an axis; uniform laminae.</p>''',
    "trap": "Dividing the moment by the area of the whole disc rather than by the area that is left. The hole is not a small correction here -- it removes a quarter of the material -- so the denominator matters as much as the numerator.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 8 — C, forces.  Terminal speed under a quadratic drag.  The question is which
#     two forces balance, not how the approach to that balance looks.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 8, "id": "S07-08", "module": "C", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Terminal speed of a falling body when the drag is proportional to the square of the speed",
    "rel": [("C", "At terminal speed the acceleration is zero, so the forces balance"),
            ("C", "The drag force rises with speed, which is why a terminal speed exists at all"),
            ("A", "Rearranging for the speed rather than the force, so the answer comes out as a square root")],
    "key": ["terminal speed", "drag", "balance", "square root"],
    "stem": '<p>A body of mass <code>m</code> falls through a fluid. The drag force on it is <code>k v<sup>2</sup></code>, where <code>v</code> is its speed and <code>k</code> is a constant.</p><p>Which expression gives its terminal speed?</p>',
    "opts": ['<code>mg</code>/<code>k</code>',
             '<code>k</code>/(<code>mg</code>)',
             '&#8730;(<code>k</code>/(<code>mg</code>))',
             '<code>mg</code>/<code>k</code><sup>2</sup>',
             '&#8730;(<code>mg</code>/<code>k</code>)'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['solves the balance equation as though the drag were proportional to the speed rather than to its square',
                    'inverts the ratio, which would be the drag coefficient needed for a given terminal speed',
                    'inverts the ratio <i>and</i> takes the root, which is the reciprocal of the right answer',
                    'takes the square root of <code>k</code> instead of of the whole fraction',
                    'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "at terminal speed the body is not accelerating, so the drag must equal the weight"),
            ("solve", "write that balance as k v<sup>2</sup> = mg"),
            ("solve", "divide by k and take the square root, which is where the root in the answer comes from"),
            ("check", "confirm the speed rises when the body is heavier and falls when the fluid is thicker, as the expression says"),
        ],
        "relations": ["k v<sup>2</sup> = m g",
                      "v<sup>2</sup> = m g / k",
                      "v = &#8730;(m g / k)"],
        "insight": "Terminal speed is a statement about forces, not about motion: it is the speed at which the two forces on the body cancel, so the acceleration stops. The whole of the algebra is one equation. What makes the question worth asking is the square root -- a quadratic drag law puts a root in the answer, and a question that ignores the root is a question about linear drag.",
        "shape": "algebraic-elimination",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "sqrt(m*g/k)", "want": "sqrt(m*g/k)"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know what terminal speed <i>is</i>. It is not a property of the object; it is the speed at which the drag has grown enough to cancel the weight, so that the net force and therefore the acceleration are zero.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the condition.</b> At terminal speed the body falls at a constant speed, so its acceleration is zero. By Newton's second law the resultant force is zero, and the only two forces are the weight downwards and the drag upwards:</p>
<div class="formula">drag = weight</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — write the balance with the given drag law.</b> The question says the drag is <code>k v<sup>2</sup></code>, and the weight is <code>mg</code>:</p>
<div class="formula">k v<sup>2</sup> = m g</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — solve for the speed.</b> Divide by <code>k</code> and then take the square root:</p>
<div class="formula">v<sup>2</sup> = m g / k
v = &#8730;(m g / k)</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The expression must have the dimensions of a speed. <code>mg</code> is a force, in kg m s<sup>-2</sup>; <code>k</code> has units of kg m<sup>-1</sup> (the units that make <code>kv<sup>2</sup></code> a force); their ratio is m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>, and the root of that is m s<sup>-1</sup>. A speed. It also behaves correctly: a heavier body has a larger terminal speed and a thicker fluid (larger <code>k</code>) has a smaller one.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>mg/k</b> is the answer for a drag proportional to <code>v</code> rather than to <code>v<sup>2</sup></code>. It is a speed by the same dimensional argument, which is what makes it tempting -- but it does not come from this drag law.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>k/(mg)</b> inverts the ratio. It has the dimensions of an inverse speed, so it cannot be a speed at all.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#8730;(k/(mg))</b> both inverts and takes the root, giving the reciprocal of the correct answer.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>mg/k<sup>2</sup></b> takes the square root of <code>k</code> alone and leaves it in the denominator, so the units come out wrong.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> terminal speed; Newton's second law with a velocity-dependent force; rearranging a quadratic balance.</p>''',
    "trap": "Treating terminal speed as the speed at which the drag <i>starts</i>, or as something to be found from the motion rather than from the forces. It is defined by a balance, and the balance is the only equation needed.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 9 — C, statics.  A cylinder in a V-groove whose two faces are at different
#     angles.  The steeper face carries LESS, which is the counter-intuitive bit.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 9, "id": "S07-09", "module": "C", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A cylinder resting in a V-groove with faces at 60 degrees and 30 degrees: the force on the steeper face, which is the smaller of the two",
    "rel": [("C", "Each face pushes perpendicular to itself, so the two normals are tilted away from the vertical by the angle each face makes with the horizontal"),
            ("C", "Resolving horizontally, the two horizontal components must cancel, which fixes the ratio of the two forces"),
            ("C", "Resolving vertically, the two vertical components must carry the whole weight")],
    "key": ["statics", "resolution", "normal reaction", "groove"],
    "stem": '<p>A uniform cylinder of weight <code>W</code> rests in a V-groove. The left face of the groove is inclined at 60&#176; to the horizontal and the right face at 30&#176;, as the diagram shows. Both faces are smooth.</p>{{FIG:s07-09}}<p>Which expression gives the force exerted by the left face?</p>',
    "opts": ['<code>W</code>/&#8730;3',
             '<code>W</code>/2',
             '<code>W</code>&#8730;3/2',
             '<code>W</code>',
             '2<code>W</code>/&#8730;3'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['puts the steeper face on the larger share, which is the natural guess and the wrong way round',
                    'correct',
                    'swaps the two faces, so it gives the force on the shallower face instead',
                    'splits the weight evenly between the two faces, which would be right only if they were at the same angle',
                    'resolves the weight along the steeper face rather than perpendicular to it'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "note that each face pushes along its own normal, so the left face pushes at 30 degrees to the vertical and the right face at 60 degrees"),
            ("solve", "resolve horizontally: the two horizontal components are equal and opposite, which relates the two forces"),
            ("solve", "resolve vertically: the two vertical components together carry W"),
            ("solve", "substitute the ratio from the first equation into the second and solve for the force on the left face"),
            ("check", "confirm the steeper face carries the smaller share, and that the two shares add up to the weight vertically"),
        ],
        "relations": ["horizontal: N_left sin 60&#176; = N_right sin 30&#176;",
                      "vertical: N_left cos 60&#176; + N_right cos 30&#176; = W",
                      "N_left = N_right / &#8730;3",
                      "N_left = W/2"],
        "insight": "The face that looks like it is doing the work is doing the least. A steep face is nearly parallel to the weight, so it pushes almost horizontally, and a horizontal push has to be paid for by the other face. The result is that the 60-degree face carries exactly half the weight and the 30-degree face carries the other 0.87 -- and that is true whatever the weight is.",
        "shape": "diagram-geometry",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "W/2", "want": "W/2"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you resolve along the right directions. A normal reaction acts perpendicular to the surface it comes from, so the angle a face makes with the horizontal is <i>not</i> the angle its force makes with the vertical -- it is the complement. Getting that the right way round is the whole question.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the two normals and their angles.</b> The left face rises at 60&#176; to the horizontal, so its normal is at 60&#176; to the <i>vertical</i>... read carefully: a normal is perpendicular to the face, so it is at 30&#176; to the vertical and 60&#176; to the horizontal. The right face rises at 30&#176;, so its normal is at 60&#176; to the vertical and 30&#176; to the horizontal. The two normals are at 30&#176; and 60&#176; to the vertical, and they point up and inwards.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — resolve horizontally.</b> There is no horizontal acceleration, so the two horizontal components cancel:</p>
<div class="formula">N_left sin 60&#176; = N_right sin 30&#176;
N_left (&#8730;3/2) = N_right (1/2)
N_left = N_right / &#8730;3</div>
<p>So the left face already carries the smaller force. Everything after this only fixes its size.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — resolve vertically.</b> The two vertical components together carry the weight:</p>
<div class="formula">N_left cos 60&#176; + N_right cos 30&#176; = W
N_left (1/2) + N_right (&#8730;3/2) = W</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — substitute and solve.</b> Put <code>N_right = &#8730;3 N_left</code> from Step 2:</p>
<div class="formula">N_left (1/2) + &#8730;3 N_left (&#8730;3/2) = W
N_left (1/2) + N_left (3/2) = W
2 N_left = W
N_left = W/2</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The right face carries <code>N_right = &#8730;3 N_left = W&#8730;3/2 &#8776; 0.87W</code>. The two add up to <code>0.50W + 0.87W = 1.37W</code>, which is more than <code>W</code> -- and that is right, because they are not parallel: only their vertical components add, and those come to <code>W</code> exactly. As a second check, if both faces were at 45&#176; each would carry <code>W/&#8730;2 &#8776; 0.71W</code>; tilting one face steeper must increase the load on the other, and <code>0.87 &gt; 0.71</code>.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>W/&#8730;3</b> gives the steeper face the larger share. It is the natural guess -- a steeper wall looks more load-bearing -- and it is backwards, because a steeper face pushes more nearly horizontally and so carries less of the weight.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>W&#8730;3/2</b> is the force on the <i>shallower</i> face. It is a real quantity in this problem and not the one asked for.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>W</b> splits the weight evenly between the faces, which would be right only if both faces were at the same angle.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2W/&#8730;3</b> resolves the weight along the steeper face instead of perpendicular to it, which is the direction a block would slide in rather than the direction the face pushes.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> normal reactions; resolving a weight into components along two given directions; three forces in equilibrium.</p>''',
    "trap": "Reading the angle off the face instead of off the normal. A face at 60 degrees to the horizontal pushes at 30 degrees to the vertical, and taking the two to be the same swaps the two forces -- which is exactly what the first two distractors do.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 10 — D, circular motion.  A rolling wheel: the velocity of the top point is
#      doubled, but its ACCELERATION is not.  That is the discriminator.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 10, "id": "S07-10", "module": "D", "diff": 1,
    "topic": "A wheel rolling at constant speed: the acceleration of the point at the top of the rim, which is the same magnitude as at every other point",
    "rel": [("D", "Every point of a rolling wheel moves in a circle about the centre, so every point has an acceleration of v²/r towards the centre"),
            ("D", "The centre itself does not accelerate, because the wheel rolls at constant speed"),
            ("A", "Separating the velocity of a point, which is a sum of the translational and rotational parts, from its acceleration, which is not")],
    "key": ["rolling", "centripetal", "acceleration", "top point"],
    "stem": '<p>A wheel of radius <code>r</code> rolls along a level road without slipping, at a constant speed <code>v</code>.</p>{{FIG:s07-10}}<p>What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the point at the top of the wheel?</p>',
    "opts": ['0',
             '<code>2v<sup>2</sup></code>/<code>r</code>',
             '<code>v<sup>2</sup></code>/(<code>2r</code>)',
             '<code>v<sup>2</sup></code>/<code>r</code>',
             '<code>4v<sup>2</sup></code>/<code>r</code>'],
    "ans": 3,
    "distractors": ['takes the constant speed of the wheel to mean the point on the rim is not accelerating, which confuses the wheel with a body that is not rotating',
                    'squares the top point&#8217;s speed, which is 2v, and then uses it in v<sup>2</sup>/r -- the classic conflation of velocity and acceleration',
                    'halves the answer, which comes from using the diameter in place of the radius',
                    'correct',
                    'doubles the answer, which comes from treating the centripetal acceleration and the translational acceleration as if they added'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "note that the wheel rolls at constant speed, so its centre has no acceleration"),
            ("relate", "every point of the rim moves in a circle about the centre, at the same angular speed"),
            ("solve", "the acceleration of any rim point is therefore directed towards the centre with magnitude v<sup>2</sup>/r"),
            ("check", "confirm that the top point is not special: only its velocity is doubled, not its acceleration"),
        ],
        "relations": ["a = v<sup>2</sup>/r for a point moving in a circle of radius r",
                      "the centre of the wheel has zero acceleration",
                      "every rim point has the same |a|, directed towards the centre"],
        "insight": "Velocity and acceleration do not obey the same rules in a rolling body. The velocity of a point is the vector sum of the translational and rotational parts, which is why the top of the wheel moves at 2v and the contact point at zero. The acceleration is not a sum of that kind: it is simply the centripetal acceleration towards the centre, and every point of the rim has the same magnitude of it.",
        "shape": "superposition",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
        "figure_support": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "v**2/r", "want": "v**2/r"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you keep a rolling body's <i>velocity</i> and its <i>acceleration</i> apart. The famous fact about rolling is that the top of the wheel moves at twice the speed of the wheel and the contact point does not move at all. The temptation is to carry that doubling over to the acceleration. It does not apply.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is the wheel doing?</b> It rolls without slipping at a constant speed. The centre of the wheel therefore moves in a straight line at a steady speed, so <b>the centre has no acceleration</b>.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — what is a point on the rim doing?</b> In the frame of the wheel, every rim point goes round the centre in a circle of radius <code>r</code>, once per revolution. The angular speed is the same for every point on the rim, because the wheel is rigid. So relative to the centre, every rim point has a centripetal acceleration</p>
<div class="formula">a = v<sup>2</sup>/r  directed towards the centre</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — put the two together.</b> The acceleration of a rim point in the frame of the road is the acceleration of the centre plus its acceleration relative to the centre. The centre contributes zero, so</p>
<div class="formula">a_top = v<sup>2</sup>/r, directed straight down towards the centre</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: D.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer must not depend on which point of the rim you pick, because the question could have asked about any of them and the physics would be the same. <code>v<sup>2</sup>/r</code> is indeed the same for all of them; <code>2v<sup>2</sup>/r</code> would single out the top, which the physics does not. Dimensional check: <code>v<sup>2</sup>/r</code> is m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup> over m, which is m s<sup>-2</sup>, an acceleration.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>0</b> is what you get by thinking of the wheel as a body moving at constant speed. A point on the rim is going round a circle, and anything going round a circle is accelerating even at constant speed.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2v<sup>2</sup>/r</b> uses the top point's <i>speed</i>, which really is <code>2v</code>, in the formula for acceleration. The velocity of the top point is a sum of the translational and rotational parts; the acceleration is not.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>v<sup>2</sup>/(2r)</b> uses the diameter where the radius belongs.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>4v<sup>2</sup>/r</b> adds a centripetal acceleration to a translational one that does not exist, because the centre is not accelerating.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> rolling without slipping; centripetal acceleration; the difference between the velocity and the acceleration of a point on a rotating body.</p>''',
    "trap": "Doubling the acceleration because the top point's speed is doubled. The velocity of a point on a rolling wheel is a sum of two contributions and its acceleration is not; the contact point has zero velocity and the same acceleration as the top.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 11 — E, materials.  A rod held at both ends and heated: the stress that
#      appears when expansion is prevented.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 11, "id": "S07-11", "module": "E", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A rod clamped at both ends and heated: the thermal stress, which is the stress needed to undo the expansion that could not happen",
    "rel": [("E", "A temperature rise would change the length by αLΔT if the rod were free to expand"),
            ("E", "Young's modulus is the stress per unit strain, so the stress needed to cancel a strain is E times it"),
            ("A", "The length cancels, so the stress does not depend on how long the rod is")],
    "key": ["thermal expansion", "Young's modulus", "stress", "clamped"],
    "stem": '<p>A metal rod is clamped firmly at both ends so that it cannot change length, and its temperature is then raised by <code>&#916;T</code>. The metal has linear expansivity <code>&#945;</code> and Young&#8217;s modulus <code>E</code>.</p><p>Which expression gives the stress that appears in the rod?</p>',
    "opts": ['<code>E</code>&#916;<code>T</code>/&#945;',
             '&#945;&#916;<code>T</code>/<code>E</code>',
             '<code>E</code>&#945;/&#916;<code>T</code>',
             '<code>E</code>&#945;&#916;<code>T</code><sup>2</sup>',
             '<code>E</code>&#945;&#916;<code>T</code>'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['inverts the role of the expansivity and the temperature, which leaves the stress independent of how hot the rod gets',
                    'inverts the whole expression, giving a strain rather than a stress',
                    'divides by the temperature change instead of multiplying by it, so the stress falls as the rod gets hotter',
                    'squares the temperature change, which is what happens when the thermal strain is multiplied by the stress rather than used as a strain',
                    'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "imagine the rod free to expand, so that it would grow by a fraction &#945;&#916;T of its length"),
            ("relate", "the clamps force it back to its original length, so the strain it is put under is &#945;&#916;T, of the opposite sign"),
            ("solve", "stress is Young's modulus times strain, so the stress is E&#945;&#916;T"),
            ("check", "confirm the length has cancelled, so a short rod and a long rod develop the same stress"),
        ],
        "relations": ["free expansion: &#916;L = &#945; L &#916;T",
                      "strain = &#916;L/L = &#945;&#916;T",
                      "stress = E &#215; strain",
                      "stress = E&#945;&#916;T"],
        "insight": "The trick is to split the process into two imaginary stages: let the rod expand freely, then push it back to its original length. The first stage gives a strain, and the second stage converts that strain into a stress through Young's modulus. Because a strain is a ratio of two lengths, the length of the rod never appears in the answer -- which is why the same rail and a model of it would develop the same stress.",
        "shape": "algebraic-elimination",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "E*(alpha*L*dT/L)", "want": "E*alpha*dT"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can see a problem about a rod that cannot move as two imaginary problems about a rod that can. That is the standard way into every thermal-stress question, and it removes the need for any cleverness about forces.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — let the rod expand freely.</b> If the clamps were not there, the rod would grow by</p>
<div class="formula">&#916;L = &#945; L &#916;T</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — push it back.</b> The clamps hold the rod at its original length, so the rod is now compressed by exactly that amount. The strain it carries is</p>
<div class="formula">strain = &#916;L / L = &#945; L &#916;T / L = &#945;&#916;T</div>
<p>The length has cancelled. That is not a coincidence: a strain is a ratio of two lengths, and the thermal expansion is proportional to the length, so the two always cancel.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — turn the strain into a stress.</b> Young's modulus is defined as stress over strain, so</p>
<div class="formula">stress = E &#215; strain = E&#945;&#916;T</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Take a steel rod, <code>&#945; = 1.2 &#215; 10<sup>-5</sup> K<sup>-1</sup></code> and <code>E = 2.0 &#215; 10<sup>11</sup> Pa</code>, heated by 50 K. The stress is <code>2.0 &#215; 10<sup>11</sup> &#215; 1.2 &#215; 10<sup>-5</sup> &#215; 50 = 1.2 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> Pa</code>, which is over a hundred megapascals -- a serious stress, and the right sort of size for why railway rails are laid with gaps. Also note that the answer does not mention the length or the cross-sectional area, and neither of those should matter: the stress is the same in a short rod and a long one.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>E&#916;T/&#945;</b> swaps the expansivity and the temperature. It would make a metal with a tiny expansivity develop an enormous stress, which is backwards.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#945;&#916;T/E</b> is the strain divided by the modulus, so it has the dimensions of strain over stress rather than stress.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>E&#945;/&#916;T</b> divides by the temperature change, so heating the rod more would <i>reduce</i> the stress. It is a real formula in this problem only in the sense that it has the right letters in it.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>E&#945;&#916;T<sup>2</sup></b> squares the temperature change, which comes from using the thermal expansion as though it were a stress rather than a strain.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> linear thermal expansion; strain as a ratio of lengths; Young's modulus; the two-stage method for thermal stress.</p>''',
    "trap": "Looking for the force in the rod. The question asks for a stress, and a stress is a strain multiplied by a modulus -- the length and the area never enter, which is why the answer has no geometry in it at all.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 12 — F, waves.  The speed of sound in a gas, compressed at constant
#      temperature.  Pressure up, density up, speed unchanged -- and the reason
#      is that the two effects cancel exactly.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 12, "id": "S07-12", "module": "F", "diff": 1,
    "topic": "The speed of sound in a gas compressed at constant temperature: it does not change, because the density rises in step with the pressure",
    # NOTE the form of the radical here.  A `rel` label is rendered as PLAIN TEXT --
    # assets/app.js escapes it, because priority.js reuses the label as a topic NAME --
    # so an entity in this field reaches the candidate verbatim as `&#8730;`.  Every
    # other field of the question is injected as HTML and takes the numeric reference.
    # The two policies are not an inconsistency: they follow the two renderers.  G12
    # enforces the plain-text one; the G3 form lint enforces the HTML one, and it
    # deliberately does not look at this field.
    "rel": [("F", "The speed of sound in a gas is √(γP/ρ), so it depends on the ratio of pressure to density and not on either alone"),
            ("F", "Compressing at constant temperature raises the pressure and the density by the same factor"),
            ("A", "Recognising a ratio that is unchanged when both of its parts are multiplied by the same number")],
    "key": ["sound", "pressure", "density", "ratio"],
    "stem": '<p>The speed of sound in a gas is given by <code>v = &#8730;(&#947;P/&#961;)</code>, where <code>&#947;</code> is a constant for the gas, <code>P</code> is its pressure and <code>&#961;</code> is its density.</p><p>A fixed mass of the gas is compressed <b>at constant temperature</b> until its pressure is twice what it was. What happens to the speed of sound in it?</p>',
    "opts": ['It doubles.',
             'It is unchanged.',
             'It is halved.',
             'It rises by a factor of &#8730;2.',
             'It falls by a factor of &#8730;2.'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['reads the speed as proportional to the pressure and ignores the density, which is the single most common slip with this formula',
                    'correct',
                    'assumes the density doubles while the pressure stays put, which would be a compression at constant pressure -- not what is described',
                    'takes the square root of the factor of two in the pressure but forgets that the density also changes',
                    'assumes the density rises while the pressure does not, and then takes the root of the wrong side of the ratio'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "note that the speed depends on the ratio P/&#961;, not on P on its own"),
            ("relate", "at constant temperature, halving the volume doubles the pressure and also doubles the density, because the mass is fixed"),
            ("solve", "the ratio P/&#961; is therefore multiplied by 2/2, which is 1"),
            ("check", "confirm that the gas constant relation P = &#961;RT/m gives P/&#961; = RT/m, which depends on the temperature alone"),
        ],
        "relations": ["v = &#8730;(&#947;P/&#961;)",
                      "P &#8594; 2P and &#961; &#8594; 2&#961; at constant temperature",
                      "P/&#961; is unchanged",
                      "so v is unchanged"],
        "insight": "The formula has two variables in it and the question changes both. Compressing a gas at constant temperature raises the pressure and the density by exactly the same factor, because the mass is fixed and the volume falls. So the ratio is untouched and the speed of sound depends only on the temperature -- which is why a gas has one speed of sound for a given temperature and not one for each pressure.",
        "shape": "ratio-cancellation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "sqrt(gamma*(2*P)/(2*rho))", "want": "sqrt(gamma*P/rho)"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you look at what a formula contains before deciding what a change does to it. The speed of sound depends on the <i>ratio</i> of pressure to density, and a constant-temperature compression changes both by the same factor.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — read the formula.</b></p>
<div class="formula">v = &#8730;(&#947;P/&#961;)</div>
<p>The pressure appears, and so does the density. Any question that changes one of them without asking about the other is a trap.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — what happens to the density?</b> The gas is compressed at constant temperature, so the volume falls and the pressure rises -- the two go together, which is Boyle's law. The mass is fixed, so if the volume halves the density doubles. The pressure was doubled by the compression, so</p>
<div class="formula">P &#8594; 2P  and  &#961; &#8594; 2&#961;</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — put both changes in.</b></p>
<div class="formula">v_new = &#8730;(&#947; &#215; 2P / (2&#961;)) = &#8730;(&#947;P/&#961;) = v</div>
<p>The factor of two appears once on the top and once on the bottom, and cancels.</p>
<p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The ideal gas law says <code>P = &#961;RT/m</code>, so <code>P/&#961; = RT/m</code>, which depends only on the temperature. That is a much shorter route to the same conclusion, and it explains the result: for a given gas, the speed of sound is fixed by the temperature and by nothing else. It also tells you what would change it -- heat the gas and the speed rises as the square root of the absolute temperature, which is why sound travels faster on a hot day.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>It doubles.</b> Reading <code>v</code> as proportional to <code>P</code> ignores the density entirely, and the square root as well.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>It is halved.</b> Assumes the density doubles while the pressure does not, which describes a different process.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>It rises by &#8730;2.</b> Takes the square root of the pressure factor and forgets that the density changed too. This is the most defensible wrong answer, because the square root is genuinely there -- it is just operating on a ratio that has not moved.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>It falls by &#8730;2.</b> The same mistake with the density on the wrong side.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the speed of sound in a gas; the ideal gas law; why pressure and density move together at constant temperature.</p>''',
    "trap": "Changing one variable in a formula that contains two. The compression doubles the density as well as the pressure, and an answer that mentions &#8730;2 has used one of them and dropped the other.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 13 — F, waves.  Beats, and the direction of the change.  Loading a fork lowers
#      its frequency, so the direction of the beat change identifies the unknown.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 13, "id": "S07-13", "module": "F", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Beats between two tuning forks: the direction the beat rate moves when one fork is loaded tells you which side of the known frequency the unknown lies on",
    "rel": [("F", "The beat rate is the difference between the two frequencies, so it gives the size of the gap and not its sign"),
            ("F", "Loading a fork with wax lowers its frequency, because it adds mass without adding stiffness"),
            ("A", "Using the direction of a change to choose between the two candidates that the size of the gap leaves open")],
    "key": ["beats", "frequency", "direction", "tuning fork"],
    "stem": '<p>A tuning fork of frequency 512 Hz is sounded beside a second fork of unknown frequency. <b>Four</b> beats per second are heard.</p><p>Wax is then put on the 512 Hz fork, which lowers its frequency, and the beat rate falls to <b>two</b> per second.</p><p>What is the frequency of the second fork?</p>',
    "opts": ['508 Hz', '516 Hz', '510 Hz', '514 Hz', '520 Hz'],
    "ans": 0,
    "distractors": ['correct',
                    'takes the other of the two candidates, which is the one the direction of the change rules out',
                    'answers with the new beat rate as though it were the frequency difference from the original fork',
                    'adds the beat rate to the waxed fork&#8217;s frequency rather than to the original one',
                    'doubles the beat rate, which comes from confusing the beat rate with the difference in the frequencies of the two forks before and after'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "note that four beats per second means the two frequencies differ by 4 Hz, which leaves two candidates"),
            ("relate", "work out what waxing does: it lowers the 512 Hz fork, so its new frequency is below 512 Hz"),
            ("solve", "test the candidate above 512: if the unknown were 516 Hz the gap would grow, so the beat rate would rise"),
            ("solve", "test the candidate below 512: if the unknown were 508 Hz the gap could fall to 2 Hz, which is what is heard"),
            ("check", "confirm that a waxed fork at 510 Hz is 2 Hz below 512, and that waxing a fork by that little is plausible"),
        ],
        "relations": ["|f - 512| = 4, so f = 516 or f = 508",
                      "waxing lowers the 512 Hz fork, so f_waxed &lt; 512",
                      "if f = 516 then |516 - f_waxed| &gt; 4, so the beats would increase",
                      "if f = 508 and f_waxed = 510 then |508 - 510| = 2, which matches"],
        "insight": "A beat rate gives you the size of a gap and not its direction, so on its own it can only narrow the answer to two candidates. What settles it is the second measurement, and specifically the direction it moves in. This is the general shape of a great many physics experiments: one measurement gives you a magnitude, and you need a second one that changes a known quantity to get the sign.",
        "shape": "monotonicity",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "512 - 4", "want": "508"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know that beats give you a difference and not a direction, and whether you can use a second observation to supply the missing sign. Half of the marks are in the second half of the question.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what the beat rate tells you.</b> Four beats per second means the two frequencies differ by 4 Hz. It does not say which is higher, so there are two candidates:</p>
<div class="formula">f = 512 + 4 = 516 Hz   or   f = 512 - 4 = 508 Hz</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — what waxing does.</b> Adding wax adds mass to the fork without making it stiffer, so it vibrates more slowly. The 512 Hz fork therefore drops to some frequency below 512 Hz.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — test the candidate above.</b> Suppose the unknown fork were 516 Hz. The waxed fork is now below 512 Hz, so the gap between 516 and the waxed fork is <i>larger</i> than the original 4 Hz. The beat rate would rise. It falls, so 516 Hz is wrong.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — test the candidate below.</b> Suppose the unknown fork is 508 Hz. The waxed fork has dropped from 512 Hz towards it, so the gap is now smaller than 4 Hz. It has fallen to 2 Hz, which means the waxed fork sits at</p>
<div class="formula">508 + 2 = 510 Hz</div>
<p>That is a drop of 2 Hz from 512, which is exactly what a small amount of wax would do.</p>
<p>So <b>Answer: A.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Both measurements are used and neither is contradicted. The first fixes the size of the gap at 4 Hz; the second fixes its direction. Notice also that the answer is consistent with the size of the change: a 2 Hz drop is small, so the two forks started 4 Hz apart and ended 2 Hz apart, which is the right sort of relationship for a modest amount of wax. If the waxed fork had ended at, say, 480 Hz, the beat rate would have been 28 Hz and the question would have said so.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>516 Hz</b> is the other candidate from the first measurement. It is the single most likely wrong answer, because it uses the first observation and ignores the second.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>510 Hz</b> is the frequency of the <i>waxed</i> fork, not of the unknown one. It is a real number in this problem and it answers a different question.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>514 Hz</b> adds the new beat rate to the original fork's frequency, mixing a measurement taken before the wax with one taken after.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>520 Hz</b> comes from doubling the beat rate or adding both beat rates together.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> beats as a difference in frequency; the effect of loading a tuning fork; using a change of sign to choose between two candidates.</p>''',
    "trap": "Stopping at 516 Hz. The first measurement leaves two candidates and the second is what decides between them; an answer that uses only the beat rate has used half the question.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 14 — G, optics.  A plane mirror.  The least height is half the person's height
#      and does not depend on how far away they stand.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 14, "id": "S07-14", "module": "G", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "The least height of a plane mirror in which a person can see their whole reflection, which is half their height and independent of the distance",
    "rel": [("G", "A plane mirror reflects with the angle of incidence equal to the angle of reflection, so a ray from a point reaches the eye along a path that is symmetric about the mirror"),
            ("G", "The mirror only needs to span the heights of the two extreme points of the image as seen from the eye"),
            ("A", "Using the geometry of similar triangles, so that the distance from the mirror cancels")],
    "key": ["plane mirror", "reflection", "similar triangles", "half height"],
    "stem": '<p>A person of height 1.70 m stands in front of a vertical plane mirror and wants to see their whole body, from the top of their head to the soles of their feet.</p>{{FIG:s07-14}}<p>What is the least height of mirror that will do this?</p>',
    "opts": ['1.70 m',
             '0.43 m',
             '0.85 m',
             '0.57 m',
             'It depends on how far the person stands from the mirror.'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['takes the mirror to need to be as tall as the person, which is the intuitive answer and is twice the truth',
                    'uses a quarter of the height, which comes from halving twice instead of once',
                    'correct',
                    'takes a third of the height, which is the answer if the eye is treated as being at the very top of the head and the geometry is then halved twice',
                    'assumes the distance must matter, when the similar triangles show that it cancels out entirely'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "for a plane mirror the image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front, and the same size"),
            ("relate", "draw the two extreme rays, one from the top of the head and one from the feet, each reflecting at the mirror into the eye"),
            ("solve", "for each ray the mirror surface is the perpendicular bisector of the segment from the object point to its image, so the reflection point is halfway in height"),
            ("solve", "the mirror therefore needs to span from halfway between the eye and the top of the head to halfway between the eye and the feet"),
            ("check", "note that the two halves add up to half the person's height, and that no distance appears in the working"),
        ],
        "relations": ["image distance behind the mirror = object distance in front",
                      "reflection point is halfway between the eye and the object point, in height",
                      "top segment = (height - eye height)/2, bottom segment = eye height/2",
                      "mirror height = (height - eye height)/2 + eye height/2 = height/2"],
        "insight": "The answer is half the person's height, and the two things that make it surprising are worth keeping. It does not depend on the distance from the mirror, because every ray scales with the distance and the scaling cancels; and it does not depend on where the eye sits, because the two half-segments always add back to half the height. A mirror half your height works from any distance, which is why a full-length mirror is a luxury and not a necessity.",
        "shape": "diagram-geometry",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
        "figure_support": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "1.70/2", "want": "0.85"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you draw the geometry rather than guess from experience. Almost everyone's instinct is that the mirror must be nearly as tall as you are, and the geometry says it need be only half.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — where is the image?</b> A plane mirror puts the image as far behind the mirror as the object is in front of it, and the image is the same size and the right way up. So there is a virtual person standing behind the glass, mirrored, at the same distance.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — trace the two extreme rays.</b> For the person to see the top of their head, a ray must leave the top of the head, reflect off the mirror, and enter the eye. For the feet, the same. Each ray obeys the law of reflection, so each one goes to the eye along a path that is symmetric about the mirror.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — find where each ray meets the mirror.</b> For the ray from the top of the head to the eye, the mirror lies halfway between the head and the eye <i>in the direction perpendicular to the mirror</i>. That means the reflection point is at a height halfway between the eye and the top of the head. Likewise, the reflection point for the feet is at a height halfway between the eye and the feet.</p>
<div class="formula">top of the mirror:  (head height + eye height)/2
bottom of the mirror: (eye height + 0)/2</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the height of the mirror is the difference.</b></p>
<div class="formula">mirror height = (head - eye)/2 + (eye - 0)/2 = head/2</div>
<p>The eye height has cancelled. So the mirror needs to be half the person's height:</p>
<div class="formula">1.70 m / 2 = 0.85 m</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer has the dimensions of a length, as it must. It does not contain the distance from the mirror, and that is the surprising part -- but it is right: walk backwards and every ray simply lengthens in proportion, so the reflection points do not move. And the mirror's position is determined too: its bottom edge sits halfway between the eye and the floor, and its top edge halfway between the eye and the top of the head. Those two positions depend on where the eye is, but the <i>gap</i> between them does not.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.70 m</b> is the instinctive answer: the mirror as tall as the person. It is what a shop selling full-length mirrors wants you to believe.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>0.43 m</b> is a quarter of the height, which comes from halving twice instead of once.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>0.57 m</b> is a third of the height, which is what you get if you halve twice -- for instance by treating the eye as being at the top of the head and then halving again.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>It depends on the distance</b> is the trap for anyone who reasons from experience instead of from the diagram. The similar triangles show that the distance cancels exactly, so the answer is the same however far away the person stands.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the law of reflection; the position of an image in a plane mirror; similar triangles in ray diagrams.</p>''',
    "trap": "Reasoning from experience rather than from the diagram. The mirror needs to be half your height, its position depends on where your eye is, and the distance you stand at cancels out of the working completely.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 15 — G, optics.  The resolution of a telescope.  The angle is a ratio, so the
#      answer is a power of ten and the physics is one line.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 15, "id": "S07-15", "module": "G", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "The smallest angle a telescope objective can resolve, which is set by the ratio of the wavelength to the diameter",
    "rel": [("G", "Two sources closer together than about λ/D cannot be told apart, because their diffraction patterns overlap"),
            ("G", "The resolving angle is a ratio of the wavelength to the aperture, so a bigger aperture resolves a smaller angle"),
            ("A", "Dividing two lengths to get a dimensionless angle, and then reading the powers of ten off the answer")],
    "key": ["diffraction", "aperture", "resolution", "wavelength"],
    "stem": '<p>The smallest angle that a telescope objective of diameter <code>D</code> can just resolve is about <code>&#955;/D</code>, where <code>&#955;</code> is the wavelength of the light.</p><p>A telescope has an objective 0.10 m in diameter and is used with light of wavelength 5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-7</sup> m. What is the smallest angle it can just resolve?</p>',
    "opts": ['5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-7</sup> rad',
             '1.1 &#215; 10<sup>-5</sup> rad',
             '2.8 &#215; 10<sup>-6</sup> rad',
             '5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-6</sup> rad',
             '5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-5</sup> rad'],
    "ans": 3,
    "distractors": ['leaves the diameter out of the division, so the answer is the wavelength itself',
                    'uses the radius in place of the diameter, which halves the denominator and doubles the angle',
                    'halves the correct answer, which comes from dividing by the diameter and then by two as well',
                    'correct',
                    'multiplies by ten instead of dividing, so the powers of ten go the wrong way'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the resolving angle is the wavelength divided by the diameter, so it is a ratio of two lengths"),
            ("solve", "write the two lengths with their powers of ten separated from the digits"),
            ("solve", "divide the digits, 5.5 by 0.10, and subtract the exponents"),
            ("check", "confirm the answer is dimensionless, as an angle in radians is, and that it is far smaller than a degree"),
        ],
        "relations": ["&#952; &#8776; &#955;/D",
                      "&#955; = 5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-7</sup> m, D = 1.0 &#215; 10<sup>-1</sup> m",
                      "&#952; = (5.5/1.0) &#215; 10<sup>-7+1</sup> rad",
                      "&#952; = 5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-6</sup> rad"],
        "insight": "There is nothing to look up and nothing to remember beyond the one ratio. The skill being tested is handling the powers of ten without a calculator: 10<sup>-7</sup> divided by 10<sup>-1</sup> is 10<sup>-6</sup>, and 5.5 divided by 1.0 is 5.5. An answer that is out by a power of ten is almost always a sign error in the exponent, not a physics error.",
        "shape": "ratio-cancellation",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "5.5e-7/0.10", "want": "5.5e-6"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can do a division in powers of ten without a calculator. The physics is given to you in the stem; the arithmetic is the question.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — write down the ratio.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#952; &#8776; &#955; / D</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — put the two lengths in the same form,</b> digits separate from powers of ten:</p>
<div class="formula">&#955; = 5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-7</sup> m
D = 0.10 m = 1.0 &#215; 10<sup>-1</sup> m</div>
<p>Writing the diameter as <code>1.0 &#215; 10<sup>-1</sup></code> rather than as <code>0.10</code> is the whole trick: it makes the exponent explicit so it cannot be lost.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — divide the digits and subtract the exponents.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#952; = (5.5 / 1.0) &#215; 10<sup>-7 - (-1)</sup> = 5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-6</sup> rad</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: D.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> An angle in radians is a ratio of two lengths, so it should have no units -- and it does not. It should also be small: a radian is about 57 degrees, so <code>5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-6</sup></code> rad is about <code>3 &#215; 10<sup>-4</sup></code> degrees, roughly a second of arc. That is the right order for a small amateur telescope, which is reassuring rather than accidental: the wavelength of light is about half a micrometre and the aperture is a tenth of a metre, and their ratio is a few millionths.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-7</sup> rad</b> is the wavelength itself, so the division by the diameter has been left out entirely.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.1 &#215; 10<sup>-5</sup> rad</b> uses the radius, 0.050 m, instead of the diameter. The denominator halves and the angle doubles.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.8 &#215; 10<sup>-6</sup> rad</b> is exactly half the right answer, which comes from dividing by the diameter and then halving again for no reason.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>5.5 &#215; 10<sup>-5</sup> rad</b> is ten times too big: the exponent has been added rather than subtracted, so the division went the wrong way.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the diffraction limit of a circular aperture; radians as a ratio of lengths; multiplying and dividing powers of ten.</p>''',
    "trap": "Losing the exponent. Writing the diameter as 0.10 rather than as 1.0 &#215; 10<sup>-1</sup> makes it easy to divide 5.5 by 10 and come out a factor of ten wrong; the answer is a power of ten either way, which is why the distractors are powers of ten too.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 16 — H, circuits.  A rate chain in kilowatt-hours.  The estimate is the point:
#      the numbers are approximate on purpose and the units carry the answer.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 16, "id": "S07-16", "module": "H", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "The cost of running a heater, built as a chain of rates: power, then hours, then days, then a price per kilowatt-hour",
    "rel": [("H", "Electrical power in kilowatts multiplied by time in hours gives energy in kilowatt-hours"),
            ("H", "The unit of the price fixes the unit of the energy, which is what stops the answer being out by a factor of a thousand"),
            ("A", "Chaining rates: a rate per hour, a number of hours per day, and a number of days, each multiplying the last")],
    "key": ["power", "kilowatt-hour", "cost", "rate chain"],
    "stem": '<p>A heater is rated at 2.0 kW and is used for about 3.0 hours a day. Electricity costs 15 p per kilowatt-hour.</p><p>Roughly what does it cost to run for 30 days?</p>',
    "opts": ['&#163;9', '&#163;27', '&#163;54', '&#163;90', '&#163;270'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['costs a single day, leaving out the thirty, or divides where it should multiply',
                    'correct',
                    'doubles the answer, which is what happens if the daily use is read as 6.0 hours or the price as 30 p',
                    'uses 100 p to the pound instead of the correct number, or multiplies the days by the hours twice',
                    'forgets to convert from pence to pounds, so the answer is in pence'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the energy used is the power in kilowatts multiplied by the time in hours, which gives kilowatt-hours"),
            ("solve", "find the energy for one day: 2.0 kW for 3.0 hours"),
            ("solve", "multiply by 30 days to get the energy for the month"),
            ("solve", "multiply by the price per kilowatt-hour, and convert the pence to pounds"),
        ],
        "relations": ["energy per day = 2.0 kW &#215; 3.0 h = 6.0 kWh",
                      "energy per month &#8776; 6.0 &#215; 30 = 180 kWh",
                      "cost = 180 &#215; 15 p = 2700 p",
                      "2700 p = &#163;27"],
        "insight": "The price is quoted per kilowatt-hour, so the unit of the answer is fixed before any arithmetic is done: you must have kilowatt-hours, not joules and not kilowatts. That is why the whole question is a chain of rates rather than a formula. And because the use is described as about three hours a day, the answer is an estimate -- the point is the order of magnitude, not the last penny.",
        "shape": "units-consistency",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "2.0*3.0*30*15/100", "want": "27"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can build a chain of rates and keep the units straight. There is no formula to remember; the units tell you what to multiply by what.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what unit does the answer need?</b> The price is 15 p per <b>kilowatt-hour</b>. So the energy must come out in kilowatt-hours, and a kilowatt-hour is simply a kilowatt multiplied by an hour -- which is why the power is given in kilowatts and the time in hours.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the energy for one day.</b></p>
<div class="formula">2.0 kW &#215; 3.0 h = 6.0 kWh per day</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the energy for the month.</b></p>
<div class="formula">6.0 kWh per day &#215; 30 days &#8776; 180 kWh</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the cost.</b></p>
<div class="formula">180 kWh &#215; 15 p per kWh = 2700 p</div>
<p>and 2700 p is &#163;27.</p>
<p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> &#163;27 a month for one heater is the right sort of figure for a domestic electricity bill, which is the check that matters most here. Notice also that the answer never needed the number of joules in a kilowatt-hour: the price was quoted per kilowatt-hour, so working in kilowatt-hours kept the whole calculation in the units the price was given in. Converting to joules first would have meant converting back, and would have been three and a half million times bigger in the middle for no benefit.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#163;9</b> is one day's cost times ten, or three days' cost -- some part of the chain has been dropped.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#163;54</b> doubles the answer, which happens if the daily use is read as 6.0 hours or the price as 30 p.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#163;90</b> comes from using 100 p to the pound instead of the correct number of pence.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>&#163;270</b> forgets to convert from pence to pounds at all, so the answer is really 2700 p read as pounds.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> electrical power and energy; the kilowatt-hour; chaining rates; converting pence to pounds.</p>''',
    "trap": "Converting to joules. The price is per kilowatt-hour, so the energy should be found in kilowatt-hours; converting to joules and back is where the powers of ten get lost, and where the answer becomes a number no one can sanity-check.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 17 — H, circuits.  Two identical cells in parallel.  The e.m.f. does not add
#      but the internal resistance halves, and that is the whole question.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 17, "id": "S07-17", "module": "H", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Two identical cells in parallel driving a resistor: the e.m.f. stays at one cell's value while the internal resistance halves",
    "rel": [("H", "Cells in parallel have the same e.m.f. as one of them, because they are connected the same way round across the same two nodes"),
            ("H", "Their internal resistances are in parallel, so two equal ones give half of one"),
            ("H", "Ohm's law applied to the whole circuit, with the internal resistance in series with the load")],
    "key": ["cells", "parallel", "internal resistance", "current"],
    "stem": '<p>Two identical cells are connected in parallel. Each has an electromotive force of 1.5 V and an internal resistance of 1.0 &#937;. The combination is connected across a resistor of 2.0 &#937;, as the diagram shows.</p>{{FIG:s07-17}}<p>What current flows in the 2.0 &#937; resistor?</p>',
    "opts": ['1.5 A', '0.50 A', '0.75 A', '0.60 A', '0.30 A'],
    "ans": 3,
    "distractors": ['reads the e.m.f. as 3.0 V and the internal resistance as 2.0 &#937;, which is the answer for the cells in series',
                    'adds the internal resistances instead of combining them in parallel, so the circuit resistance comes to 4.0 &#937;',
                    'combines the internal resistances correctly but doubles the e.m.f. as well',
                    'correct',
                    'uses only one cell, ignoring the second one altogether'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "note that two identical cells in parallel have the same e.m.f. as one of them, not twice it"),
            ("solve", "combine the two internal resistances in parallel to get the resistance of the source"),
            ("solve", "add the load resistance in series to get the resistance of the whole circuit"),
            ("solve", "apply Ohm's law with the single e.m.f. and the total resistance"),
            ("check", "confirm the current is less than the short-circuit current of one cell, 1.5 A, as it must be"),
        ],
        "relations": ["e.m.f. of the combination = 1.5 V",
                      "internal resistance = 1.0 &#937; in parallel with 1.0 &#937; = 0.50 &#937;",
                      "total resistance = 0.50 + 2.0 = 2.5 &#937;",
                      "I = 1.5 / 2.5 = 0.60 A"],
        "insight": "Putting cells in parallel is not the same as putting them in series. In parallel they are both connected across the same two nodes, so neither can raise the potential difference above the other's e.m.f. -- what they do instead is share the current, which is equivalent to halving the internal resistance. So the current rises, but only from 1.5/3.0 to 1.5/2.5, which is a half, not a doubling.",
        "shape": "circuit-reduction",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "1.5/(1.0/2 + 2.0)", "want": "0.6"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know what cells in parallel do. The instinct is to add the electromotive forces, which is what happens in series; in parallel the e.m.f. stays put and the internal resistance falls.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is the e.m.f. of the pair?</b> Both cells are connected between the same two nodes, the same way round. Each one on its own would hold those nodes 1.5 V apart. Neither can hold them further apart than the other, so the pair holds them at 1.5 V. <b>The e.m.f. does not add.</b></p>
<p><b>Step 2 — what is the internal resistance of the pair?</b> The two internal resistances sit between the same two nodes, so they are in parallel:</p>
<div class="formula">r = (1.0 &#215; 1.0)/(1.0 + 1.0) = 0.50 &#937;</div>
<p>Two equal resistors in parallel give half of one. This is the physical meaning of "the cells share the current": each supplies half, so each drops half the internal voltage it would have dropped alone.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the whole circuit.</b> The source resistance is in series with the load:</p>
<div class="formula">R_total = 0.50 + 2.0 = 2.5 &#937;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — Ohm's law.</b></p>
<div class="formula">I = 1.5 / 2.5 = 0.60 A</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: D.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The current must be more than it would be with one cell alone, which is <code>1.5/3.0 = 0.50 A</code>, and it is: 0.60 A. It must be less than the short-circuit current of one cell, <code>1.5/1.0 = 1.5 A</code>, because the load is still there, and it is. So the answer lies where it should. It has also risen by only 20 per cent, not by a factor of two -- which is the right shape for a change that only affects the internal resistance.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.5 A</b> is the answer for the two cells in <i>series</i>: <code>3.0 V</code> across <code>2.0 + 1.0 + 1.0 = 4.0 &#937;</code>. It is a real current in this circuit, just from the wrong circuit.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>0.50 A</b> adds the internal resistances instead of combining them in parallel, giving <code>2.0 + 1.0 + 1.0 = 4.0 &#937;</code> with a 1.5 V e.m.f. It is also the current from a single cell.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>0.75 A</b> combines the resistances correctly but doubles the e.m.f. as well, so it takes both of the two things that could change and gets one of them wrong.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>0.30 A</b> uses one cell but adds the other cell's resistance, which no connection of two cells produces.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> cells in parallel and in series; internal resistance; Ohm's law for a whole circuit.</p>''',
    "trap": "Adding the electromotive forces. Two identical cells in parallel give one cell's e.m.f. and half its internal resistance; only cells in series add their e.m.f.s.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 18 — H, circuits.  A potentiometer.  The balance length is proportional to the
#      e.m.f., so two balances give a ratio and nothing else.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 18, "id": "S07-18", "module": "H", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A potentiometer used to compare two e.m.f.s: the balance lengths are in the same ratio as the e.m.f.s, so the wire's length and its resistance never enter",
    "rel": [("H", "On a uniform current-carrying wire the potential difference per unit length is the same everywhere"),
            ("H", "At balance no current is drawn from the cell being tested, so the balance length measures its e.m.f. and not its terminal p.d."),
            ("A", "Using the ratio of two balance lengths, so that the driving cell and the wire's resistance cancel")],
    "key": ["potentiometer", "balance", "e.m.f.", "ratio"],
    "stem": '<p>A potentiometer is set up with a uniform resistance wire 1.00 m long. When a standard cell of e.m.f. 1.5 V is connected, the balance point is found at 45.0 cm from the left-hand end. When a cell of unknown e.m.f. is connected instead, the balance point is at 60.0 cm.</p>{{FIG:s07-18}}<p>What is the unknown e.m.f.?</p>',
    "opts": ['2.0 V', '1.1 V', '1.8 V', '2.5 V', '3.0 V'],
    "ans": 0,
    "distractors": ['correct',
                    'subtracts the two balance lengths and uses the difference, which is a length and not a fraction of the wire',
                    'inverts the ratio of the balance lengths, so the unknown comes out smaller than the standard cell',
                    'uses the whole 1.00 m in place of the standard cell&#8217;s balance length, which is a length ratio with nothing to do with the e.m.f.s',
                    'adds the two balance lengths, so the unknown comes out larger than the wire can measure'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "on a uniform wire the p.d. per centimetre is the same all along it, so the p.d. is proportional to the balance length"),
            ("relate", "at balance the cell draws no current, so the balance length is proportional to its e.m.f."),
            ("solve", "form the ratio of the two balance lengths and set it equal to the ratio of the two e.m.f.s"),
            ("solve", "multiply the standard e.m.f. by that ratio"),
            ("check", "confirm the unknown is larger than the standard cell, since its balance length is longer"),
        ],
        "relations": ["E_unknown / E_standard = L_unknown / L_standard",
                      "L_unknown = 60.0 cm, L_standard = 45.0 cm",
                      "E_unknown = 1.5 &#215; 60.0/45.0",
                      "E_unknown = 2.0 V"],
        "insight": "A potentiometer is a comparison, not a measurement, and that is its strength. The driving cell, the wire's resistance per metre and its total length all drop out of the ratio, so none of them has to be known to any accuracy. What is left is two lengths, and the whole question is whether you form the ratio the right way up.",
        "shape": "proportionality",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
        "figure_support": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "1.5*60.0/45.0", "want": "2.0"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you understand what a balance point measures. It measures the potential difference along a stretch of a uniform wire, and because the wire is uniform, that potential difference is proportional to the length.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — why the length is proportional to the e.m.f.</b> A steady current flows through the whole wire, so the potential difference per centimetre is the same everywhere along it. A length <code>L</code> therefore has a potential difference proportional to <code>L</code>, with the constant of proportionality set by the driving cell and the wire's resistance per metre.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — why that measures an e.m.f. and not a terminal p.d.</b> At balance the galvanometer reads zero, so no current is drawn from the cell being tested. With no current there is no voltage lost across its internal resistance, and the potential difference it holds is its full e.m.f. That is the whole point of the instrument.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — form the ratio.</b> The same constant of proportionality applies to both measurements, so it cancels:</p>
<div class="formula">E_unknown / E_standard = L_unknown / L_standard</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — substitute and solve.</b></p>
<div class="formula">E_unknown = 1.5 V &#215; 60.0 / 45.0
E_unknown = 1.5 &#215; 4/3 = 2.0 V</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: A.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The unknown's balance point is further along the wire, so its e.m.f. must be larger than 1.5 V, and 2.0 V is. The ratio 60.0/45.0 is 4/3, so the answer is a third larger than the standard cell, which is the right size for a 1.5 V cell measured against a 2.0 V one. Note also that the 1.00 m length of the wire never entered the working: both balance points are well inside it, and the length only matters in that it must be long enough to reach both.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.1 V</b> uses the <i>difference</i> of the two balance lengths, 15.0 cm, as a fraction of something. The difference is a length, and the ratio that matters is of lengths to the same wire.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.8 V</b> inverts the ratio, using 45.0/60.0, which makes the unknown smaller than the standard cell even though its balance point is further along.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.5 V</b> uses the whole metre in place of the standard cell's balance length.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>3.0 V</b> adds the two balance lengths, which puts the unknown's balance point beyond the end of the wire.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the potentiometer; balance and the null method; proportionality along a uniform wire.</p>''',
    "trap": "Using the balance lengths as lengths rather than as a ratio. The absolute lengths are irrelevant -- only their ratio is -- and an answer that has used 1.00 m or the difference of the two has used a length where a fraction of the wire was wanted.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 19 — I, capacitors.  Charging a capacitor from a battery.  Half the energy the
#      battery supplies is lost in the resistor, whatever the resistance is.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 19, "id": "S07-19", "module": "I", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Charging a capacitor from a battery through a resistor: half of the energy the battery supplies ends up in the resistor, whatever the resistance is",
    "rel": [("I", "A capacitor of capacitance C charged to a potential difference V stores ½CV²"),
            ("I", "The charge that passes through the battery is CV, so the battery supplies energy QV = CV²"),
            ("C", "The difference between the energy supplied and the energy stored must have gone into the resistor as heat")],
    "key": ["capacitor", "energy", "charging", "fraction"],
    "stem": '<p>A capacitor of capacitance <code>C</code> is charged from a battery of e.m.f. <code>V</code> through a resistor of resistance <code>R</code>, until it is fully charged.</p>{{FIG:s07-19}}<p>What fraction of the energy supplied by the battery is dissipated in the resistor?</p>',
    "opts": ['1/4', '3/4', '1', 'It depends on <code>R</code>.', '1/2'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['confuses the energy with the charge, which divides equally between the capacitor and the resistor only in the sense that both carry the same charge',
                    'reports the fraction that reaches the capacitor rather than the fraction lost, inverting the question',
                    'assumes all the energy supplied ends up stored, which would need the resistance to be zero and would still be wrong',
                    'expects the resistance to matter, when in fact it sets how long the charging takes and not how much energy is lost',
                    'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the charge that passes through the battery is the final charge on the capacitor, Q = CV"),
            ("relate", "the battery supplies energy QV for that charge, since every coulomb is lifted through the full e.m.f."),
            ("solve", "the energy stored in the capacitor is &#189;CV<sup>2</sup>, which is half of CV<sup>2</sup>"),
            ("solve", "the difference, &#189;CV<sup>2</sup>, must have been dissipated in the resistor"),
            ("check", "note that R does not appear anywhere in the working, so the fraction cannot depend on it"),
        ],
        "relations": ["Q = C V",
                      "energy supplied by the battery = Q V = C V<sup>2</sup>",
                      "energy stored = &#189; C V<sup>2</sup>",
                      "energy dissipated = C V<sup>2</sup> - &#189; C V<sup>2</sup> = &#189; C V<sup>2</sup>"],
        "insight": "The factor of a half that appears in &#189;CV<sup>2</sup> is the same factor of a half that appears here, and it is not a coincidence. As the capacitor charges, the potential difference across it rises from zero to V while the battery holds a steady V, so the average potential difference the charge is lifted through is V/2 for the capacitor and V for the battery. The difference is lost in the resistor. The resistance changes how fast this happens and nothing else.",
        "shape": "conservation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": False,
        "figure_support": True,
        "scope_note": "Recorded judgement, not a silent pass: the question is about *charging*, which the official note names when it excludes 'time dependent charging'. The exclusion is about the time behaviour -- RC, the time constant, the exponential -- and this question is built so that R cancels: the answer is 1/2 whatever the resistance, and the trap is exactly the candidate who looks for R. The physics tested is energy bookkeeping, 1/2 CV^2 against the CV^2 the battery supplies, which is the same capacitor-energy content already shipped in sections 2 and 6. The note's own alternative -- 'a knowledge that Q = CV' -- is the first relation in the solution and is what fixes the charge that passes through the battery.",
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym",
              "got": "(C*V**2 - C*V**2/2)/(C*V**2)",
              "want": "1/2"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can account for energy honestly in a circuit that is not in a steady state. The battery supplies one amount, the capacitor stores another, and the difference has to be somewhere -- it is in the resistor, as heat.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the charge that moves.</b> When the capacitor is fully charged it holds</p>
<div class="formula">Q = C V</div>
<p>That charge has passed through the battery, because the circuit is a single loop.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the energy the battery supplies.</b> The battery lifts every coulomb through its full e.m.f. of <code>V</code>, from the moment the first one moves to the moment the last one does. So</p>
<div class="formula">energy supplied = Q V = C V<sup>2</sup></div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the energy the capacitor stores.</b></p>
<div class="formula">energy stored = &#189; C V<sup>2</sup></div>
<p>The factor of a half is there because the potential difference across the capacitor <i>rises</i> from zero to <code>V</code> as it charges, so on average each coulomb is lifted through only <code>V/2</code>.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — the difference.</b> Whatever is not stored must have been dissipated in the resistor:</p>
<div class="formula">energy dissipated = C V<sup>2</sup> - &#189; C V<sup>2</sup> = &#189; C V<sup>2</sup></div>
<p>As a fraction of what was supplied:</p>
<div class="formula">(&#189; C V<sup>2</sup>) / (C V<sup>2</sup>) = 1/2</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The resistance does not appear anywhere in the working, and that is the surprising and correct result: <code>R</code> sets how long the charging takes, not how much energy is lost. A large <code>R</code> means a small current for a long time and the same total heat; a small <code>R</code> means a large current briefly. Doubling the battery's e.m.f. quadruples both the energy supplied and the energy lost, so the fraction stays at a half. The answer is a pure number, as a fraction must be.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>1/4</b> is a quarter, which is what you get by squaring the half somewhere -- for instance by comparing the stored energy with the square of the supplied energy.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>3/4</b> inverts the question and reports the fraction that <i>reaches the capacitor</i>, not the fraction lost.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>1</b> assumes everything supplied is dissipated, which would leave the capacitor uncharged.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>It depends on R</b> is the trap for anyone who thinks the answer should mention every quantity in the question. It does not: the resistance is a rate-setter, not an energy-taker.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> energy stored in a capacitor; the energy supplied by a battery; conservation of energy in a circuit that is charging.</p>''',
    "trap": "Looking for the resistance. The answer is one half and the resistance is not in it, because R sets how long the charging takes rather than how much energy is lost -- and an answer that mentions R has answered a question about time.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 20 — J, thermal.  A falling ball warms up.  The mass cancels, which is what
#      makes the answer a temperature and not an energy.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 20, "id": "S07-20", "module": "J", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A lead ball dropped from a height: the rise in temperature, in which the mass cancels and only the height and the specific heat capacity matter",
    "rel": [("J", "The gravitational potential energy lost is mgh, and it all becomes internal energy on impact"),
            ("J", "The internal energy that raises a body's temperature by ΔT is mcΔT"),
            ("A", "Equating the two, so that the mass appears on both sides and cancels")],
    "key": ["specific heat", "gravitational energy", "temperature rise", "cancellation"],
    "stem": '<p>A lead ball is dropped from rest from a height of 20 m onto a hard surface. Assume that all of the energy it has on impact goes into internal energy in the ball.</p><p>Lead has a specific heat capacity of 130 J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>, and <code>g</code> may be taken as 10 m s<sup>-2</sup>. Roughly what is the rise in the ball&#8217;s temperature?</p>',
    "opts": ['0.15 K', '1.0 K', '1.5 K', '2.0 K', '15 K'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['divides by the height instead of multiplying, so the answer is the reciprocal of the right one',
                    'takes the specific heat capacity as 200 J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>, or divides by the height as well as by the capacity',
                    'correct',
                    'multiplies by <code>g</code> a second time, or uses 200 m in place of 20 m',
                    'forgets to convert the energy per kilogram into a temperature, so the answer is ten times too large'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the ball has gravitational potential energy mgh before the drop and none after"),
            ("relate", "that energy becomes internal energy, and the internal energy needed to raise the temperature is mc&#916;T"),
            ("solve", "equate the two and cancel the mass, which is what makes the answer independent of how heavy the ball is"),
            ("solve", "put the numbers in: &#916;T = gh/c"),
            ("check", "confirm the answer is a temperature and that lead, with a small specific heat capacity, warms up noticeably"),
        ],
        "relations": ["m g h = m c &#916;T",
                      "&#916;T = g h / c",
                      "&#916;T &#8776; 10 &#215; 20 / 130",
                      "&#916;T &#8776; 1.5 K"],
        "insight": "The mass cancels, and that is the point of the question. A heavier ball has more energy to lose and more material to heat, in exactly the same proportion, so the temperature rise depends only on the height and on the material. It is the same reason a feather and a hammer fall at the same rate in a vacuum: the property that supplies the energy and the property that absorbs it are the same property.",
        "shape": "ratio-cancellation",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "10*20/130", "want": "1.5384615384615385"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can set up an energy balance and then notice that one of the quantities cancels. The cancellation is the physics: it is why the answer does not depend on the mass of the ball.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the energy that arrives.</b> The ball falls through a height <code>h</code>, losing gravitational potential energy</p>
<div class="formula">E = m g h</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — the energy that raises the temperature.</b> Heating a mass <code>m</code> of a material of specific heat capacity <code>c</code> by <code>&#916;T</code> needs</p>
<div class="formula">E = m c &#916;T</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — equate and cancel.</b> If all of the first becomes the second:</p>
<div class="formula">m g h = m c &#916;T
&#916;T = g h / c</div>
<p>The mass has gone. That is not a mathematical convenience; it says that a heavy ball and a light one dropped from the same height warm up by the same amount, because the heavier one both has more energy and needs more energy for the same rise.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — put the numbers in.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#916;T &#8776; (10 &#215; 20) / 130 = 200/130 &#8776; 1.5 K</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The answer is a temperature, and the units confirm it: <code>gh/c</code> is (m s<sup>-2</sup>)(m) / (J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>), and a joule per kilogram is m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>, so the whole thing is K. The size is also right: a rise of a degree and a half is small enough that you would not notice it by hand, which matches experience -- dropped objects do warm up, but only slightly. Lead warms more than most metals because its specific heat capacity is low, and a material with a specific heat capacity ten times larger would warm by a tenth as much.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>0.15 K</b> divides by the height where it should multiply, so the answer is the reciprocal of the right one.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.0 K</b> rounds the specific heat capacity to 200, or divides by the height as well as by the capacity.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.0 K</b> multiplies by <code>g</code> twice, or uses a height of 200 m.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>15 K</b> is ten times too big, which happens if the energy per kilogram is read as a temperature without dividing by the specific heat capacity.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> gravitational potential energy; specific heat capacity; energy balances in which the mass cancels.</p>''',
    "trap": "Hunting for the mass. It is not given and it is not needed, because it cancels -- and an answer that looks for it has missed the reason the question can be asked at all.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 21 — J, thermal.  A tyre warmed by a journey.  The gauge is not a pressure
#      gauge in the sense the gas law needs: it reads the excess over the
#      atmosphere, and the atmosphere is a constant added to something that grows.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 21, "id": "S07-21", "module": "J", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A sealed tyre warmed by a journey: the gauge pressure rises by a larger fraction than the temperature, because the gauge shows only the excess over the atmosphere",
    "rel": [("J", "At constant volume the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is proportional to its absolute temperature"),
            ("J", "A ratio of temperatures is only meaningful on the absolute scale, so both must be converted from Celsius"),
            ("A", "A gauge reads the excess over atmospheric pressure, so the atmosphere has to be added before the gas law is applied and subtracted again afterwards")],
    "key": ["gas law", "absolute temperature", "gauge pressure", "ratio"],
    "stem": '<p>A car tyre is inflated on a cold morning, when the air inside it is at 20 &#176;C, until the pressure gauge reads 2.0 atm. After a long drive the air inside has warmed to 50 &#176;C. The volume of the tyre has not changed.</p><p>Atmospheric pressure is 1.0 atm. Roughly what does the gauge read now?</p>',
    "opts": ['1.7 atm', '2.0 atm', '2.2 atm', '2.3 atm', '3.3 atm'],
    "ans": 3,
    "distractors": ['inverts the temperature ratio, so the pressure falls as the tyre warms -- the answer that comes from dividing by 323/293 instead of multiplying by it',
                    'treats the pressure as fixed because the volume is fixed, which is the wrong reading of what "constant volume" implies',
                    'scales the gauge reading by the temperature ratio without first adding the atmosphere, so it scales only the part of the pressure that is not atmospheric',
                    'correct',
                    'reports the absolute pressure, having applied the gas law correctly and then forgotten to subtract the atmosphere again'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the tyre is rigid, so the volume of the air inside is fixed and p/T is constant"),
            ("relate", "both temperatures must be put on the absolute scale before they are divided"),
            ("relate", "the gauge reads the excess over atmospheric pressure, so the pressure the law applies to is 2.0 + 1.0 = 3.0 atm"),
            ("solve", "apply p2 = p1 T2/T1 to the absolute pressures"),
            ("solve", "evaluate the ratio 323/293 as one plus 30/293, which is about 1.10"),
            ("solve", "multiply: 3.0 x 1.10 = 3.3 atm absolute"),
            ("check", "subtract the atmosphere to return to the gauge reading and note that it has risen by about 0.3 atm"),
        ],
        "relations": ["p / T = constant at fixed volume",
                      "T(K) = theta(C) + 273",
                      "p_absolute = p_gauge + p_atmosphere",
                      "p2 = p1 T2 / T1 = 3.0 &#215; 323/293",
                      "p2(gauge) = p2(absolute) - 1.0"],
        "insight": "The gauge is an offset instrument, and an offset instrument distorts a ratio. The air inside is at 3.0 atm absolute and rises by a tenth of that, which is 0.3 atm; but the gauge only ever showed 2.0, so the same 0.3 atm is a sixth of what it shows. The temperature rose by 10% and the gauge rose by 15%, and no gas law was broken -- the offset did it. This is the same reason a small change in atmospheric pressure moves a barometer reading by more than it moves the true pressure.",
        "shape": "proportionality",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "(2.0+1.0)*323/293 - 1.0", "want": "2.3071672354948807"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you keep the two pressures apart. A tyre gauge does not measure the pressure of the air inside; it measures how much more there is than the air outside. The gas law applies to the first and the gauge shows the second, and the whole question lives in that gap.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what is held fixed.</b> The tyre does not change shape, so the volume of the air inside is constant. For a fixed mass of gas at constant volume the pressure is proportional to the absolute temperature:</p>
<div class="formula">p / T = constant</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — put the temperatures on the absolute scale.</b> A ratio of temperatures is only meaningful in kelvin, because the Celsius scale starts somewhere arbitrary:</p>
<div class="formula">T1 = 20 + 273 = 293 K
T2 = 50 + 273 = 323 K</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the gauge is not the pressure.</b> The gauge reads the excess over the atmosphere, so the pressure that the gas law applies to is</p>
<div class="formula">p1 = 2.0 + 1.0 = 3.0 atm  (absolute)</div>
<p>This is the step the question is built on. The gauge says 2.0, but the air inside is pushing with 3.0, and it is the 3.0 that grows with temperature.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — apply the law.</b></p>
<div class="formula">p2 / p1 = T2 / T1,   so   p2 = 3.0 &#215; 323/293</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — evaluate the ratio.</b> The temperature has risen by 30 K on a base of 293 K, so the ratio is one plus a small fraction:</p>
<div class="formula">323/293 = 1 + 30/293 &#8776; 1 + 0.10 = 1.10</div>
<p>That is the small-change approximation doing its usual work: a rise of about a tenth in absolute temperature gives a rise of about a tenth in absolute pressure.</p>
<div class="formula">p2 &#8776; 3.0 &#215; 1.10 = 3.3 atm  (absolute)</div>
<p><b>Step 6 — back to the gauge.</b> The gauge still reads the excess over the atmosphere, so subtract it again:</p>
<div class="formula">p2(gauge) = 3.3 - 1.0 = 2.3 atm</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: D.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The absolute pressure rose from 3.0 to 3.3, which is 10% and matches the 10% rise in absolute temperature. The gauge, though, went from 2.0 to 2.3, which is 15%. The gas obeyed the law exactly; it is the offset that changed the percentage. That is worth noticing, because it is the reason the answer is 2.3 and not 2.2. And the direction is right: a tyre really does gain a few tenths of an atmosphere on a long drive, which is why pressures are specified for cold tyres.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.7 atm</b> inverts the temperature ratio. Dividing by 1.10 instead of multiplying gives 2.7 absolute, which is 1.7 on the gauge -- a tyre that cools as it warms.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.0 atm</b> reads "the volume is constant" as "the pressure is constant". A fixed volume is exactly the condition under which the pressure <i>must</i> rise.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.2 atm</b> scales the gauge reading alone: 2.0 &#215; 1.10 = 2.2. It is the answer to a question in which the atmosphere does not exist, and it is the most tempting wrong answer here.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>3.3 atm</b> is the absolute pressure. The working is right and the last step is missing, which is why it is a plausible option rather than an absurd one.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the gas laws at constant volume; the kelvin scale; gauge and absolute pressure.</p>''',
    "trap": "Doing the gas law on the gauge reading. The gauge measures an excess, not a total, so a 10% rise in the gas&#8217;s own pressure becomes a 15% rise on the dial -- and the answer that skips the offset, 2.2 atm, is the one that looks tidiest.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 22 — K, nuclear.  A decay curve with a constant background added to it.  The
#      recorded curve does not halve, because what halved has been offset.  The
#      figure is the question: the flattening at the right-hand end is the tell.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 22, "id": "S07-22", "module": "K", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A decay curve recorded with a steady background: the background must be subtracted before the curve can be read, and only then does it halve",
    "rel": [("K", "The recorded count rate is the source’s own rate plus a background that does not decay"),
            ("K", "The half-life is the time for the source’s own rate to fall by a factor of two, whatever the detector records"),
            ("A", "Recognising a ratio as a power of two, which turns the fall into a number of half-lives")],
    "key": ["half-life", "background", "decay curve", "ratio"],
    "stem": '<p>A radioactive source is placed close to a detector, and the count rate recorded by the detector is plotted against time. The laboratory has a steady background count rate of 200 counts per minute from other sources, and this does not change while the measurement is made.</p>{{FIG:s07-22}}<p>Roughly what is the half-life of the source?</p>',
    "opts": ['10 s', '20 s', '30 s', '40 s', '80 s'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['takes each 20-second interval on the graph as two half-lives, halving the true answer',
                    'correct',
                    'ignores the background and asks where the recorded curve has fallen to half of its starting value, which happens at about 30 s',
                    'reads the point at which the recorded curve reaches a quarter of its starting value, which the background pushes out to about 40 s',
                    'takes the whole plotted range as a single half-life, reading the graph as a straight line from its first value to its last'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the rate the detector records is the source&#8217;s own rate plus the background, and only the first of those decays"),
            ("relate", "the background must therefore be subtracted from every reading before the curve is read as a half-life"),
            ("solve", "subtract it at the two ends of the plot: the source alone falls from 840 - 200 = 640 to 240 - 200 = 40"),
            ("solve", "640 falls to 40, a factor of 16, and 16 is 2 to the fourth power, so four half-lives have passed"),
            ("solve", "four half-lives in 80 seconds gives 80/4 = 20 seconds each"),
            ("check", "test an interior point: after one half-life the source should be at 320, and 320 + 200 = 520, which is what the graph shows at 20 s"),
        ],
        "relations": ["recorded rate = source rate + background",
                      "source rate = recorded rate - 200",
                      "640 / 40 = 16 = 2<sup>4</sup>",
                      "half-life = 80 s / 4 = 20 s"],
        "insight": "A constant added to something that decays does not decay. The recorded curve is an exponential sitting on a floor of 200, and the floor is what flattens the right-hand end -- 640 falling to 40 becomes 840 falling to 240, a ratio of only 3.5 instead of 16. The curve is still perfectly readable, but only after the background is taken off, and the tell is that the tail stops falling. A candidate who reads the curve as drawn gets a half-life that grows as the source gets weaker, which is impossible, and that impossibility is the clue that something has been added.",
        "shape": "graph-reading",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "sym", "got": "(840-200)/(240-200)", "want": "16"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can read a graph for what it is measuring rather than for what it looks like. The detector records the source <i>and</i> the room, and the room does not decay.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — say what the detector is counting.</b> Every count that arrives during the measurement is either from the source or from somewhere else:</p>
<div class="formula">recorded rate = source rate + background</div>
<p>The background is 200 counts per minute and it is steady. The source is the only thing that changes.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — take the background off.</b> Both readings have to come off the curve first, and a curve is read to the nearest gridline, so each of them is an estimate as well as a reading: roughly 840 at the start and roughly 240 at the end. Rearranging, and putting those two in:</p>
<div class="formula">source rate = recorded rate - 200
at t = 0:    640 &#8776; 840 - 200
at t = 80 s:  40 &#8776; 240 - 200</div>
<p>Those are the numbers the curve was hiding. The recorded curve fell from 840 to 240 -- a factor of only 3.5, which is not a power of two at all and is why the curve looks wrong if it is read as drawn.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — how many halvings?</b></p>
<div class="formula">640 / 40 = 16 = 2<sup>4</sup></div>
<p>Four factors of two, so four half-lives have passed in the 80 seconds the source was observed.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — one half-life.</b></p>
<div class="formula">half-life = 80 s / 4 = 20 s</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p>
<p><b>Step 5 — check it against a point in the middle.</b> This is worth doing, because two points can be joined by any number of curves and a third settles it. At <code>t = 20 s</code>, one half-life in, the source should be at 320 counts per minute. Adding the background back gives 320 + 200 = 520, and the graph shows 520 at 20 s. The reading is consistent. At <code>t = 40 s</code> the source should be at 160, so the recorded rate should be 360, which is also what the graph shows.</p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Notice what the background does to the answer. If the curve were read as drawn, 840 to 240 is a factor of 3.5, which is between two and three half-lives, so the half-life would come out somewhere near 30 s -- and it would depend on which pair of points you chose, which no half-life ever does. That dependence on the choice of points is the signature of an uncorrected background, and it is the reason a practical physicist measures the background first.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>10 s</b> halves the right answer, which is what happens if each 20-second interval is taken as two halvings rather than one.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>30 s</b> ignores the background and looks for the point where the recorded curve is half of 840. That is 420, reached at about 30 s, and it is the most common error here.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>40 s</b> reads the quarter point of the recorded curve, which the background pushes out past where it belongs.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>80 s</b> takes the whole plotted range as one half-life, which reads the graph as a straight line rather than as a decay.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> radioactive decay and half-life; background count rate; reading a decay curve; recognising a ratio as a power of two.</p>''',
    "trap": "Reading the curve as drawn. A steady background has been added to something that halves, and the sum does not halve -- it flattens. The tell is that the tail stops falling and the apparent half-life grows as the source weakens.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 23 — K, nuclear.  Where fission energy comes from: the rise in binding energy
#      per nucleon, multiplied by every nucleon.  An estimate, and the estimate
#      is the point -- the change per nucleon is under 1 MeV.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 23, "id": "S07-23", "module": "K", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "The energy released by one fission, estimated from the rise in binding energy per nucleon rather than from a remembered figure",
    "rel": [("K", "The energy released when a nucleus splits is the increase in the total binding energy of its nucleons"),
            ("K", "The total binding energy of a nucleus is the binding energy per nucleon multiplied by the number of nucleons"),
            ("A", "Estimating a product from a per-nucleon figure, which keeps the arithmetic to one multiplication")],
    "key": ["fission", "binding energy", "estimation", "MeV"],
    "stem": '<p>When a heavy nucleus of mass number 236 splits into two fragments, the binding energy per nucleon rises from 7.6 MeV in the original nucleus to 8.5 MeV in the fragments.</p><p>Roughly how much energy is released by the fission of one such nucleus?</p>',
    "opts": ['2.1 MeV', '21 MeV', '210 MeV', '240 MeV', '420 MeV'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['multiplies the change per nucleon by two, as though only the two fragments were involved rather than all 236 nucleons',
                    'misreads the change per nucleon as 0.09 MeV instead of 0.9 MeV, which is a factor of ten lost in the subtraction',
                    'correct',
                    'rounds the change per nucleon up to 1.0 MeV, or multiplies by 250 nucleons instead of 236',
                    'counts the energy twice over, as though each of the two fragments released the whole amount rather than sharing it'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the energy released is the increase in the total binding energy of the nucleons"),
            ("relate", "the total binding energy is the binding energy per nucleon multiplied by the number of nucleons"),
            ("solve", "the change per nucleon is 8.5 - 7.6 = 0.9 MeV"),
            ("solve", "multiply by the mass number: 0.9 &#215; 236"),
            ("check", "compare with the standard figure of a few hundred MeV for a fission, and confirm the order of magnitude"),
        ],
        "relations": ["energy released = increase in total binding energy",
                      "total binding energy = (binding energy per nucleon) &#215; A",
                      "change per nucleon = 8.5 - 7.6 = 0.9 MeV",
                      "E &#8776; 0.9 &#215; 236 &#8776; 210 MeV"],
        "insight": "The number worth carrying in your head is not 200 MeV; it is 0.9 MeV per nucleon. Almost every heavy nucleus that fissions releases roughly the same energy per nucleon, because the curve of binding energy against mass number is nearly flat across the heavy elements, so the energy of a fission scales with the size of the nucleus rather than depending on which nucleus it is. That is why the answer can be estimated to one significant figure from two numbers, and why a fission of a slightly smaller nucleus releases slightly less.",
        "shape": "conservation",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "0.9*236", "want": "212.4"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you know where the energy of a fission comes from, rather than remembering a number. The source is the binding energy, and the size of the release is set by how much that binding energy rises.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — say where the energy comes from.</b> The fragments are more tightly bound than the nucleus they came from. A nucleon that is more tightly bound has less energy, and the difference has to go somewhere: it becomes the kinetic energy of the fragments. So</p>
<div class="formula">energy released = increase in total binding energy</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — turn per-nucleon figures into totals.</b> The binding energy per nucleon is an average over the nucleus, so the total is that figure multiplied by the number of nucleons:</p>
<div class="formula">total binding energy = (binding energy per nucleon) &#215; A</div>
<p><b>Step 3 — the change per nucleon.</b></p>
<div class="formula">&#916;(binding energy per nucleon) = 8.5 - 7.6 = 0.9 MeV</div>
<p>Notice how small it is. Less than one MeV per nucleon, against the eight or so that each nucleon already has.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — multiply by every nucleon.</b> All 236 of them are in the fragments, and each one gains the same 0.9 MeV:</p>
<div class="formula">E &#8776; 0.9 MeV &#215; 236 &#8776; 212 MeV</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Two hundred million electronvolts is the standard order of magnitude for a fission, so the estimate has landed where it should. It is also worth seeing how large that is in everyday units: 210 MeV is about <code>3.4 &#215; 10<sup>-11</sup> J</code>, and a chemical reaction releases a few electronvolts per molecule, so a fission releases something like a million times more energy per event than a chemical reaction does. That factor of a million is why a kilogram of uranium can do what a trainload of coal does. Note too that the answer scales with the mass number, so the estimate does not depend on knowing which nucleus this is.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.1 MeV</b> multiplies the change per nucleon by two, as though only the two fragments were involved rather than every nucleon in them.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>21 MeV</b> loses a factor of ten in the subtraction, reading the change as 0.09 MeV per nucleon instead of 0.9.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>240 MeV</b> rounds the change per nucleon up to a whole 1.0 MeV, or multiplies by 250 nucleons rather than 236. Both are small slips and both push the answer the same way.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>420 MeV</b> doubles the answer, which is what happens if the two fragments are each credited with the full release.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> binding energy and mass defect; why fission releases energy; estimation from a per-nucleon figure.</p>''',
    "trap": "Multiplying by the wrong number of nucleons. The change is per nucleon and there are 236 of them, so the release is a couple of hundred MeV and not a couple -- and the smallness of the per-nucleon change is exactly what makes that multiplication necessary.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 24 — L, quantum.  THE DEEP ONE (nine moves).  The graph gives the threshold
#      frequency, which gives the work function, which is only half the job --
#      the question then asks about a wavelength the graph says nothing about.
#      Three separate pieces of physics and a unit conversion, in one item.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 24, "id": "S07-24", "module": "L", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "The photoelectric effect across three steps: the threshold frequency read from a stopping-potential graph gives the work function, and the work function then gives the kinetic energy for a stated wavelength",
    "rel": [("L", "The stopping potential is the potential that just stops the fastest electrons, so eV_s measures the maximum kinetic energy"),
            ("L", "The work function is the energy of a photon at the threshold frequency, where the emitted electrons have no kinetic energy at all"),
            ("L", "The energy of a photon of a given wavelength is hc divided by that wavelength"),
            ("A", "The joule and the electronvolt are two units for the same quantity, and the conversion has to be applied to both energies before they can be subtracted")],
    "key": ["photoelectric", "work function", "stopping potential", "threshold"],
    "stem": '<p>Light of wavelength 330 nm is incident on a metal surface. The graph shows how the stopping potential for the electrons emitted from this surface varies with the frequency of the incident light.</p>{{FIG:s07-24}}<p>Take the Planck constant to be 6.6 &#215; 10<sup>-34</sup> J s, the speed of light in a vacuum to be 3.0 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>, and the elementary charge to be 1.6 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> C.</p><p>Roughly what is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons?</p>',
    "opts": ['0 eV', '1.3 eV', '1.7 eV', '2.1 eV', '3.8 eV'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['assumes the light is at or below the threshold, so nothing is emitted -- but the wavelength is shorter than the threshold wavelength and electrons do come off, just slowly',
                    'reads the threshold frequency off the graph as 6.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz rather than 5.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz, which makes the work function larger and the kinetic energy smaller',
                    'correct',
                    'reports the work function, which is the energy needed to escape and not the energy left over after escaping',
                    'reports the energy of the photon and never subtracts the work function, which would mean the electron escaped without paying for it'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the stopping potential is what just stops the fastest electrons, so the height of the graph is the maximum kinetic energy in electronvolts"),
            ("relate", "the graph crosses the axis where the maximum kinetic energy is zero, so that crossing is the threshold frequency"),
            ("solve", "read the crossing off the graph: it lies halfway between the 4.0 and 6.0 marks, at 5.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz"),
            ("relate", "at the threshold a photon has exactly enough energy to free an electron and none to spare, so the work function is h f0"),
            ("solve", "evaluate the work function in joules"),
            ("solve", "keep both energies in joules, because converting twice would mean rounding twice"),
            ("solve", "find the energy of a 330 nm photon from hc divided by the wavelength"),
            ("solve", "subtract in joules, then convert the single result to electronvolts"),
            ("check", "subtract and confirm the answer is positive, smaller than the photon energy, and consistent with the slope of the graph"),
        ],
        "relations": ["e V_s = KE_max : the stopping potential measures the maximum kinetic energy in electronvolts",
                      "&#966; = h f<sub>0</sub> : the work function is the photon energy at the threshold frequency",
                      "E = h c / &#955; : the energy of a photon of a given wavelength",
                      "KE_max = h f - &#966; : the photoelectric equation",
                      "1 eV = 1.6 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> J"],
        "insight": "The graph does not contain the answer; it contains one number that the answer needs. A stopping-potential graph looks like it is about the electrons, and its slope is about the Planck constant, but the only thing this question asks of it is where it crosses the axis. Once the work function is out of the graph, the question becomes a different question -- a photon energy and a subtraction -- and the candidate has to notice that the wavelength in the stem has nothing to do with the frequencies on the axis. Two energies from two different sources, in two different units, and then one subtraction.",
        "shape": "graph-reading",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval",
              "expr": "6.6e-34*3.0e8/3.3e-7 - 6.6e-34*5.0e14",
              "want": "2.7e-19"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can take one number out of a graph and then leave the graph behind. The question has three separate pieces of physics in it -- a graph to read, a work function to find, and a photon energy to calculate -- and they are joined by a single subtraction.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — what the height of the graph measures.</b> The stopping potential is the potential difference that brings the fastest emitted electrons to rest. The work done on one of them is <code>e V_s</code>, so the height of the graph is the maximum kinetic energy:</p>
<div class="formula">KE_max = e V_s</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — why a graph of volts is a graph of energy.</b> The electronvolt is defined so that an electron accelerated through one volt gains one electronvolt. That is this situation in reverse: the electron climbs back through <code>V_s</code> volts and gives up all of its kinetic energy doing so. So the number on the axis can be read as an energy in electronvolts directly, with no conversion at this stage.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — where the graph crosses the axis.</b> At the crossing, <code>V_s = 0</code> and so <code>KE_max = 0</code>. The electrons come off with nothing to spare, and that frequency is the threshold. It is the only thing this question takes from the graph.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — read the crossing off it.</b> It falls between the 4.0 and 6.0 gridlines, and halfway between them is</p>
<div class="formula">f<sub>0</sub> = 5.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</div>
<p>Halfway is not a coincidence to be guessed at. The line is straight, so its crossing can be interpolated from any two points on it, and the two gridlines bracket the crossing evenly.</p>
<p><b>Step 5 — the work function.</b> At the threshold a photon has exactly enough energy to free the electron and none left over, so the whole of its energy is the work function:</p>
<div class="formula">&#966; = h f<sub>0</sub> = 6.6 &#215; 10<sup>-34</sup> &#215; 5.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> = 3.3 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> J</div>
<p><b>Step 6 — keep both energies in joules for now.</b> The answer is wanted in electronvolts, but the subtraction is easier in joules and the conversion can be done once at the end. Converting twice means rounding twice, and two roundings then have to be added. So note the work function and carry on:</p>
<div class="formula">&#966; = 3.3 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> J</div>
<p><b>Step 7 — the energy of the 330 nm photon.</b> This comes from the stem and not from the graph:</p>
<div class="formula">E = h c / &#955; = (6.6 &#215; 10<sup>-34</sup> &#215; 3.0 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup>) / 3.3 &#215; 10<sup>-7</sup>
  = 6.0 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> J</div>
<p><b>Step 8 — subtract, then convert once.</b> Whatever the photon had beyond the work function becomes kinetic energy:</p>
<div class="formula">KE_max = 6.0 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> - 3.3 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> = 2.7 &#215; 10<sup>-19</sup> J</div>
<p>Dividing by the charge on an electron turns that into electronvolts, and this is a division a candidate can do in their head: twenty-seven over sixteen is one and seven tenths to two figures, so</p>
<div class="formula">KE_max &#8776; 1.7 eV</div>
<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>
<p><b>Step 9 — check the answer three ways.</b> First, it is positive, so electrons are indeed emitted. Second, it is smaller than the photon energy, as it must be, because the electron had to pay the work function on the way out. Third, the threshold wavelength should be <code>c/f<sub>0</sub> = 3.0 &#215; 10<sup>8</sup> / 5.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> = 600 nm</code>, and the light used is 330 nm, comfortably shorter, so there is plenty of energy to spare -- which matches a kinetic energy that is a good fraction of the photon energy. Finally, the slope of the graph should be <code>h/e</code>: the line rises a little over two volts across five units of 10<sup>14</sup> Hz, which is <code>4.1 &#215; 10<sup>-15</sup> V s</code>, and multiplying by the elementary charge gives <code>6.6 &#215; 10<sup>-34</sup> J s</code>, the Planck constant the question supplied. The graph is consistent with the constants, so the reading of the crossing can be trusted.</p>
<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>
<p>&middot; <b>0 eV</b> assumes the light is at or below the threshold. It is not: the threshold wavelength is 600 nm and the light is 330 nm.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>1.3 eV</b> reads the crossing as 6.0 &#215; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz instead of 5.0. That makes the work function larger by a fifth, and it is exactly the error the interpolated axis is there to catch.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>2.1 eV</b> is the work function itself -- the price of escaping, rather than what is left after paying it.</p>
<p>&middot; <b>3.8 eV</b> is the energy of the photon in electronvolts. Reporting it means the electron escaped without paying anything, which would make the work function zero and the graph cross the axis at the origin.</p>
<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> the photoelectric equation; stopping potential and threshold frequency; the work function; photon energy from wavelength; converting between joules and electronvolts.</p>''',
    "trap": "Stopping at the work function. The graph gives the threshold, the threshold gives the work function, and the work function is not the answer -- it is one of the two numbers that have to be subtracted. The other one comes from a wavelength the graph never mentions.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 25 — L, quantum.  THE SECOND DEEP ONE (nine moves).  A photon count that has
